export const htmlPages = {
  "studylab/about-and-privacy": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by about(). -->\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">STUDYLAB / LEARNING MODULES</p>\n<h1>Built to make the logic clearer.</h1>\n<div class=\"panel theory\">\n  <h2>About the course</h2>\n  <p>\n    StudyLab (formerly LogicLab) is an independent supplementary learning platform by Lefa\n    Malatji. Its Programming Logic and Design content follows the five-unit outline\n    supplied for this project. Explanations and practice questions are original; the\n    platform is not an official institutional course or assessment.\n  </p>\n  <h2>Language and review</h2>\n  <p>\n    This first release is in English. Reviewed Sepedi explanations and additional modules\n    are planned. Teaching content and diagram behaviour should be reviewed before use in\n    assessed work.\n  </p>\n  <h2>Your progress and privacy</h2>\n  <p>\n    Lesson completion tracking is available in My progress. Earlier saved completion marks\n    are retained. Pass the required lesson checks, then mark the lesson complete. Written\n    practice tasks are self-checked against model solutions. The interactive exercises\n    check selected answers and statement order, with two progressive hints and worked\n    solutions unlocked after an attempt. Lesson answers, interactive-exercise progress,\n    written drafts and editor code save in localStorage in this browser when available.\n    Diagrams are generated locally. Tracer runtime inputs and execution history stay in\n    page memory. Practice exam answers and self-assessed written marks save in this\n    browser. A timed exam keeps its deadline when you leave the page; saved answers are\n    submitted when the exam is open or next reopened after the deadline. Practice exams\n    reuse the lesson quiz bank and are not secure assessments. Saved work does not sync\n    across devices or identify individual students sharing one browser profile. Use Saved\n    work to clear drafts and question progress on a shared device.\n  </p>\n  <h2>Practice, not secure assessment</h2>\n  <p>\n    Solutions are hidden in the interface until a complete attempt is submitted. Because\n    this is a browser-only application, a determined user can inspect downloaded source\n    files to find answers. It is not a secure examination system.\n  </p>\n  <h2>Current boundaries</h2>\n  <p>\n    The diagram lab supports a documented subset, not every pseudocode dialect. It\n    validates structure but does not prove an algorithm correct. The interactive tracer\n    runs documented variable, one-dimensional array and two-dimensional grid statements\n    with typed input and execution limits. Guided array and sorting traces use fixed\n    examples. Methods and classes are not executable in the tracer yet. The SQL workspace\n    executes SQLite locally with documented SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL compatibility\n    modes. It is not a full native server. Demo database changes are temporary; SQL drafts\n    and challenge feedback save in this browser. Check my answer compares challenge\n    results against fresh demo and edge-case data; native SQL Server scripts and written\n    explanations remain self-assessed. There are no student accounts, lecturer dashboards\n    or cross-device progress in this version.\n  </p>\n  <h2>Contact</h2>\n  <p>\n    Report a content or website issue to\n    <a\n      style=\"color: var(--purple); text-decoration: underline\"\n      href=\"mailto:lazylefa@gmail.com?subject=StudyLab%20feedback\"\n      >lazylefa@gmail.com</a\n    >.\n  </p>\n  <a class=\"button\" href=\"#home\">Back to learning</a>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/code-tools": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by toolsHub(). -->\n<h1>See how your code works.</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Use a diagram to see the structure, or trace a program one step at a time.\n</p>\n<div class=\"hub-grid\">{{dynamic_1}}</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-ado-api": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    ADO.NET is the more hands-on route. You write SQL, add typed parameters, open a\n    connection and read results yourself.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Install and create the right project</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Install Visual Studio with ASP.NET and web development and a .NET 10-compatible\n        SDK. For WPF also install .NET desktop development. Check Tools → Get Tools and\n        Features if templates are missing.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Create ASP.NET Core Empty for the Minimal API, or ASP.NET Core Web API with Use\n        controllers for a controller project. Select .NET 10. Alternatively open the\n        supplied .csproj to get exactly the lesson files.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Use a separate folder for every example. Delete the generated weather sample if it\n        conflicts with the supplied files. Restore NuGet packages before building.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        In View → Terminal, cd into the folder containing StudyLab.csproj. For local API\n        testing run dotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100. Open requests.http and use\n        Send Request above each request. Use the actual port from the console if you\n        launch with a different profile.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Your first request</h2>\n    <pre><code>GET http://localhost:5100/api/students</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Initially expect 200 OK with []. A browser address bar can send GET, but cannot\n      conveniently send all CRUD methods. Use the .http editor, Postman, curl.exe or a\n      Swagger UI. The JSON object is a data packet, not an HTML page.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prepare the database</h2>\n    <p>\n      Open setup.sql in SSMS connected to your local SQL Server. Run it once to create\n      StudyLabAdo and Students. GO is an SSMS batch separator, not a command to send\n      through SqlCommand. The schema already exists in this example, so EF migrations are\n      not used. Microsoft.Data.SqlClient supplies SqlConnection, SqlCommand and\n      SqlDataReader.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Understand the methods</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        SqlConnection describes a connection; OpenAsync actually opens it. await using\n        disposes it after the operation. Connection pooling can reuse physical\n        connections.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        SqlCommand holds the SQL and parameters. Parameters carry values separately from\n        SQL text. Never concatenate names or search input into a SQL statement.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        ExecuteReaderAsync returns rows. ReadAsync moves to the next row; GetInt32 and\n        GetString read typed columns in SELECT order.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        ExecuteScalarAsync returns one value, here the inserted identity from OUTPUT\n        INSERTED.Id. ExecuteNonQueryAsync returns affected-row information for\n        updates/deletes.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        The full controller implements all five resource routes with cancellation and\n        validation. This is an alternative to EF, not an extra layer required by EF.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Parameters and expected results</h2>\n    <pre><code>cmd.Parameters.Add(&quot;@name&quot;, SqlDbType.NVarChar, 80).Value = input.Name.Trim();\ncmd.Parameters.Add(&quot;@age&quot;, SqlDbType.Int).Value = input.Age;</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      A name containing an apostrophe should still save as text. The CRUD response\n      sequence is the same as the EF controller. If UPDATE affects zero rows, return 404\n      instead of pretending a change happened.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Fix: \"WHERE Name = '\" + input + \"'\".</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Use WHERE Name=@name and add an NVarChar parameter. Parameters are for values;\n        validate/allowlist any selectable column names separately.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>\n        Why dispose a reader before reusing the connection for another command?\n      </summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        An open reader can hold the connection busy. Finish reading and dispose it; do not\n        rely on a reader disappearing by itself.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-advanced-api": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    An advanced API is not just more endpoints. It must cope with large lists,\n    simultaneous edits, cancelled requests and users who must not see each other’s\n    records.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Install and create the right project</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Install Visual Studio with ASP.NET and web development and a .NET 10-compatible\n        SDK. For WPF also install .NET desktop development. Check Tools → Get Tools and\n        Features if templates are missing.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Create ASP.NET Core Empty for the Minimal API, or ASP.NET Core Web API with Use\n        controllers for a controller project. Select .NET 10. Alternatively open the\n        supplied .csproj to get exactly the lesson files.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Use a separate folder for every example. Delete the generated weather sample if it\n        conflicts with the supplied files. Restore NuGet packages before building.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        In View → Terminal, cd into the folder containing StudyLab.csproj. For local API\n        testing run dotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100. Open requests.http and use\n        Send Request above each request. Use the actual port from the console if you\n        launch with a different profile.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Your first request</h2>\n    <pre><code>GET http://localhost:5100/api/students</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Initially expect 200 OK with []. A browser address bar can send GET, but cannot\n      conveniently send all CRUD methods. Use the .http editor, Postman, curl.exe or a\n      Swagger UI. The JSON object is a data packet, not an HTML page.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Create the schema first</h2>\n    <pre><code>dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate\ndotnet ef database update\ndotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Install the matching dotnet-ef tool if missing. This project uses SQLite and\n      Identity tables in secure-school.db. Project files below contain the complete\n      controller, DTOs, DbContext and Program.cs.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Read the protections in the controller</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        [Authorize] requires a signed-in user. Mine filters by the current user ID for\n        every read/update/delete; choosing somebody else’s numeric ID does not bypass\n        ownership.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        StudentInput prevents overposting; StudentView exposes the intended fields. Inputs\n        are validated before saving.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        page and pageSize are bounded. OrderBy gives stable ordering before Skip/Take.\n        search narrows results. AsNoTracking avoids tracking read-only lists.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        A Version GUID is marked as an EF concurrency token. GET returns the token. PUT\n        sends the token it saw. A stale token or a racing database update returns 409\n        Conflict. Fetch again before retrying.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Async EF methods accept CancellationToken. Rate limiting bounds each authenticated\n        caller or unauthenticated source, returning 429. ProblemDetails handles unexpected\n        exceptions without exposing internal details in normal production responses.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>PUT, PATCH and transaction choices</h2>\n    <p>\n      PUT replaces the editable representation and should produce the same stored values\n      when repeated. The supplied PATCH action changes selected fields with a small\n      StudentPatch DTO and the same version check; omitted Name or Age stays unchanged.\n      PATCH changes selected fields; define a small validated patch DTO or carefully\n      validate allowed JSON Patch paths. Do not blindly expose arbitrary entity paths.\n      DELETE can return 204 the first time and 404 later while still being idempotent in\n      its effect. One EF SaveChanges is transactional for its changes. For multiple saves\n      or related operations use an explicit transaction; with retry strategies execute the\n      whole transaction through Database.CreateExecutionStrategy. Do not keep a database\n      transaction open while waiting for a remote HTTP call.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Expected checks</h2>\n    <p>\n      Without a token: 401. With a valid token: a paged object containing page, pageSize,\n      total and items. A bad input: 400. Another user’s record: 404. A stale version: 409.\n      A successful update: 200 with a new version. Test two clients loading the same\n      version, then save both; the second must conflict.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why is hiding another person’s Edit button insufficient?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        A client can send requests directly. The server must filter by ownership and\n        authorise the operation.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>When should you introduce a repository or service?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        When it gives business rules a clear, testable home or separates multiple data\n        sources. Do not add layers only to make the beginner example longer.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-api-auth": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Authentication asks “Who are you?” Authorisation asks “May you do this?” A password is\n    not a token and a token should not be treated like public text.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Follow the complete Identity flow</h2>\n    <p>\n      ASP.NET Core Identity stores hashed passwords and user records through\n      Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore. AddIdentityApiEndpoints registers\n      its API authentication services. AddEntityFrameworkStores connects Identity to\n      AppDb. MapIdentityApi under /auth maps registration, login and other account\n      endpoints. This example uses Identity’s opaque bearer tokens; they are not JWTs.\n    </p>\n    <pre><code>POST http://localhost:5100/auth/register\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{&quot;email&quot;:&quot;student@example.com&quot;,&quot;password&quot;:&quot;ChooseYourOwn_Strong123!&quot;}\n\nPOST http://localhost:5100/auth/login?useCookies=false\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{&quot;email&quot;:&quot;student@example.com&quot;,&quot;password&quot;:&quot;ChooseYourOwn_Strong123!&quot;}</code></pre>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Use the response</h2>\n    <p>\n      Register returns 200 with no useful resource body when successful. Login returns\n      accessToken, refreshToken, tokenType and expiry information. Copy the actual\n      accessToken into the token variable in requests.http. Then send Authorization:\n      Bearer followed by the token when calling /api/students. Wrong credentials should\n      return 401. Never commit your real token. Use /auth/refresh with the refresh token\n      for the supported refresh flow.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Before a real deployment</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Serve authentication over HTTPS. Local HTTP in this lab is restricted to localhost\n        experiments.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Configure confirmed email, delivery, password-reset and account recovery before\n        relying on account verification. The sample does not send emails.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        For a browser-first application consider secure HttpOnly cookies with CSRF\n        protection instead of storing bearer tokens in localStorage.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        For enterprise SSO, third-party clients or JWT/OAuth requirements use a real\n        OpenID Connect/OAuth provider and validate issuer, audience, signature and\n        lifetime. Do not invent your own token format or password encryption.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        401 means not authenticated; 403 means authenticated but lacking permission. Add\n        roles/policies for staff/admin functions, not a client-side role dropdown.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Can you decode this opaque token as a JWT?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. This Identity flow issues a proprietary protected token. Do not promise JWT\n        claims or third-party JWT interoperability.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>How do you prove ownership isolation?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Register two test accounts, create a row under the first, then try GET/PUT/DELETE\n        of that ID under the second. Expect no access to the first account’s row.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-api-testing": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Testing means checking what happens, not just noticing that a page opens. Test the\n    happy path and the mistakes that real people make.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>A repeatable request checklist</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Start the in-memory API fresh. GET the empty list. POST a valid student and record\n        the returned ID and Location header.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        GET that ID and check name/age. PUT a different name; GET again to prove\n        persistence within this process. DELETE it; GET must return 404.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        POST an invalid age, empty name, whitespace-only name and name longer than 80\n        characters; all should return 400. Send broken JSON and confirm rejection.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        For EF examples restart without deleting the database and check that kept rows\n        survive. For the secure API test 401, cross-owner access, stale versions and\n        page-size limits.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        A unit test isolates a service or validation rule. An integration test starts a\n        test host with WebApplicationFactory&lt;Program&gt; and a separate test database.\n        Never run destructive tests against a real student database.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>OpenAPI and Swagger are related, not identical</h2>\n    <p>\n      Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi can generate an OpenAPI document. AddOpenApi registers\n      generation; MapOpenApi exposes /openapi/v1.json in Development. A Swagger UI is a\n      separate viewer. Install a compatible Swagger UI package and configure its endpoint\n      to that JSON if you want an interactive web page. A .NET 10 template does not\n      promise /swagger will exist by default. The supplied .http files work without\n      Swagger.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Debug failures systematically</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        404: check exact route, HTTP method and launch port. 400: inspect validation\n        response and JSON Content-Type. 415: wrong request media type.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        500: inspect the server log and breakpoint locally. Do not return passwords, stack\n        traces or connection strings to clients.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        CS0246: missing using, file, namespace or NuGet restore. Unable to resolve\n        service: add the missing DI registration.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        No database table: run migrations. Cannot connect: check database service and\n        connection string. 401: send a valid unexpired token. 409: fetch the latest\n        version before editing.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Design one positive and one negative test for PUT.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Positive: update an existing row then GET it. Negative: an invalid age must be\n        rejected and must not change the stored row.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why is “the response was 200” not enough?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The body might contain the wrong data. Check status, headers, response shape and\n        stored state.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Run the repeatable HTTP checks</h2>\n    <p>\n      The downloadable project below is a console test runner. Start API project02 on\n      port5100 in one terminal. Open this test project in another terminal and run\n      <code>dotnet run</code>. Expected: eight PASS lines and All CRUD checks passed. Each\n      check asserts status or stored values; a failed check throws.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-async": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    When you order food, you do not need to stand frozen at the counter. An asynchronous\n    method can wait for I/O and let the thread do other useful work.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Follow a Task</h2>\n    <pre><code>static async Task&lt;string&gt; LoadAsync(string name)\n{\n    await Task.Delay(20);\n    return name;\n}\nvar values = await Task.WhenAll(LoadAsync(&quot;A&quot;), LoadAsync(&quot;B&quot;));\nConsole.WriteLine(string.Join(&quot;,&quot;, values));</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Expected: A,B. The operations overlap their waiting time. WhenAll returns results in\n      input order, not completion order. Use async Task for work and async Task&lt;T&gt;\n      for a result. async void is mainly for event handlers.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Cancellation and errors</h2>\n    <p>\n      Pass a CancellationToken into HTTP, database and delay methods. A cancelled task\n      usually throws OperationCanceledException at await. A task failure is rethrown at\n      await; handle it where you can recover. Avoid .Result or .Wait on a UI thread. Do\n      not hold a lock across await. For CPU work use a bounded amount of parallel work,\n      rather than launching unlimited tasks.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why use Task.Delay instead of Thread.Sleep in an async demo?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>Task.Delay yields while waiting. Thread.Sleep blocks the current thread.</p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Rewrite two sequential downloads as overlapping I/O.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Create both tasks first, then await Task.WhenAll. Still limit concurrency for\n        large batches.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-class-libraries": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    A class library is a toolbox another application can reuse. It normally has no Main\n    method and produces a DLL rather than its own user interface.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Two projects, one shared rule</h2>\n    <p>\n      The root StudyLab.csproj is the console app. SharedRules/SharedRules.csproj is a\n      separate class library. ProjectReference links them. Open the root project in Visual\n      Studio; the reference should restore/build with it. The root excludes SharedRules\n      source from its own compilation so the same types are not compiled twice.\n    </p>\n    <pre><code>dotnet add StudyLab.csproj reference SharedRules/SharedRules.csproj</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      The supplied project already includes that reference. MarkRules.IsPass(70) returns\n      true. Keep dependencies and visibility minimal: public exposes a reusable contract,\n      internal hides implementation details.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>When to share code</h2>\n    <p>\n      Good candidates include validation rules, calculations and domain models shared by\n      API, MVC and desktop applications. Avoid making a library depend on a specific\n      window or controller. Package versioning matters when distributing a library through\n      NuGet. Target a framework compatible with every consuming application; net10.0 does\n      not run in a .NET8 consumer just because both use C#.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>What is a practical benefit of one shared pass rule?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Fix the rule in one place and test it once, then rebuild/update the consuming\n        applications.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why not place database passwords inside the DLL?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Compiled assemblies can be inspected. Secrets belong in runtime configuration with\n        controlled access.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-code-database-first": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Code-first starts from classes and mapping. Database-first starts from an existing\n    schema. Both can use EF Core; choose the workflow that matches who controls the\n    database.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Code-first workflow</h2>\n    <pre><code>dotnet ef migrations add AddCourse\ndotnet ef database update</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Add a property to the entity, generate a migration, inspect its SQL and data-loss\n      risks, then apply it in a training database. Keep migrations in source control. Do\n      not delete a migration already applied to a shared environment just to hide a\n      mistake.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Database-first workflow</h2>\n    <pre><code>dotnet ef dbcontext scaffold &quot;Name=ConnectionStrings:School&quot; Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer --context SchoolDb --output-dir GeneratedModels --no-onconfiguring</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Configure the named connection for the project first. The command reads schema and\n      generates entity/context code. Use a clean separate project for this exercise so it\n      does not duplicate the existing SchoolDb. Put custom logic in separate/partial\n      files; regeneration can overwrite generated files. Do not hard-code credentials in\n      generated code.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Compare carefully</h2>\n    <p>\n      Code-first is convenient when your team owns the schema and wants reviewed\n      migrations. Database-first is convenient for a supplied or centrally managed schema.\n      Neither approach removes the need for keys, constraints, backups, indexes or careful\n      migration review. Expected scaffold output is a context plus entity classes matching\n      the database’s tables, not a finished UI.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>A scaffold command creates a duplicate SchoolDb. Fix it.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Run the example in a clean project or choose a distinct context/namespace and\n        remove conflicting generated definitions intentionally.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Does database-first mean CRUD must use raw SQL?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. Generated entities and a context can use ordinary EF queries and SaveChanges.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-commands-mvc": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    A command is a reusable button action with a second question: “Am I allowed to run\n    right now?”\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>ICommand parts</h2>\n    <p>\n      Execute performs the action. CanExecute decides whether the action is enabled.\n      CanExecuteChanged tells the UI to ask again. RelayCommand wraps an Action and\n      Func&lt;bool&gt; so the ViewModel does not need a new class for every action.\n    </p>\n    <pre><code>AddCommand = new RelayCommand(AddStudent, CanAddStudent);\n// When Name or AgeText changes:\nAddCommand.Refresh();</code></pre>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Build the MVC equivalent</h2>\n    <p>\n      For the web requirement, open project 07-mvc-client from Unit2. Run API02 on5100 and\n      MVC07 on5200. In MVC the controller receives a new HTTP request and returns\n      HTML/redirects. In WPF a ViewModel stays alive with the window and notifications\n      refresh bindings. MVC forms use antiforgery protection; WPF commands do not use web\n      antiforgery tokens. Both must validate data and keep reusable business rules out of\n      presentation markup.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Make an improvement</h2>\n    <p>\n      Add a Clear command that empties Name and AgeText. It should be enabled when either\n      has text. Add a SelectedStudent property and a Delete command enabled only when a\n      list item is selected. In the web version, Delete is a POST form that then asks the\n      API to DELETE the resource.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why call CanExecuteChanged after editing Name?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The button needs to recalculate whether it can run. Without notification, enabled\n        state can become stale.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Can a WPF command return a Razor View?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        That would mix UI models. A WPF command changes ViewModel state; an MVC action can\n        return a Razor View.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-crud-controllers": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    A controller puts related endpoints in one named class. It is like putting all\n    student-counter instructions in one labelled folder.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Install and create the right project</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Install Visual Studio with ASP.NET and web development and a .NET 10-compatible\n        SDK. For WPF also install .NET desktop development. Check Tools → Get Tools and\n        Features if templates are missing.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Create ASP.NET Core Empty for the Minimal API, or ASP.NET Core Web API with Use\n        controllers for a controller project. Select .NET 10. Alternatively open the\n        supplied .csproj to get exactly the lesson files.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Use a separate folder for every example. Delete the generated weather sample if it\n        conflicts with the supplied files. Restore NuGet packages before building.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        In View → Terminal, cd into the folder containing StudyLab.csproj. For local API\n        testing run dotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100. Open requests.http and use\n        Send Request above each request. Use the actual port from the console if you\n        launch with a different profile.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Your first request</h2>\n    <pre><code>GET http://localhost:5100/api/students</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Initially expect 200 OK with []. A browser address bar can send GET, but cannot\n      conveniently send all CRUD methods. Use the .http editor, Postman, curl.exe or a\n      Swagger UI. The JSON object is a data packet, not an HTML page.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Read the controller from top to bottom</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        [ApiController] turns model-validation failures into 400 responses.\n        [Route(\"api/students\")] is the shared URL prefix.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        [HttpGet], [HttpPost], [HttpPut] and [HttpDelete] choose the HTTP verb. {id:int}\n        accepts an integer route parameter.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Constructor injection supplies StudentStore from the container. Singleton means\n        one store per application process; Scoped normally means one per HTTP request;\n        Transient means a new instance when resolved.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        StudentInput uses Required, StringLength and Range from\n        System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations. A DTO limits what clients can set. Do not\n        accept an entire database entity just because it is convenient.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        CreatedAtAction uses the read-one action to build the Location URL. Action names\n        are C# method names; route names and action names are not always the same thing.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Response shape and failure cases</h2>\n    <pre><code>POST /api/students\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Lefa&quot;,&quot;age&quot;:22}</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Expected: 201 Created and {\"id\":1,\"name\":\"Lefa\",\"age\":22} on a fresh store. Invalid\n      JSON can fail before your action runs. A blank name or age outside 16-100 returns\n      400. An unknown ID returns 404. A successful PUT/DELETE returns 204; an empty\n      response is correct.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why not let POST accept an Id chosen by the user?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The server should own identity assignment. A create DTO excludes Id and prevents\n        accidental overwriting.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Add a course field safely.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Add Course to the model and input DTO, validate its length, map it in POST/PUT,\n        then add request tests. A database-backed model also needs a migration.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-crud-minimal": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    CRUD means Create, Read, Update and Delete. Start with a small in-memory list so the\n    HTTP ideas are easy to see before adding a database.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Install and create the right project</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Install Visual Studio with ASP.NET and web development and a .NET 10-compatible\n        SDK. For WPF also install .NET desktop development. Check Tools → Get Tools and\n        Features if templates are missing.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Create ASP.NET Core Empty for the Minimal API, or ASP.NET Core Web API with Use\n        controllers for a controller project. Select .NET 10. Alternatively open the\n        supplied .csproj to get exactly the lesson files.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Use a separate folder for every example. Delete the generated weather sample if it\n        conflicts with the supplied files. Restore NuGet packages before building.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        In View → Terminal, cd into the folder containing StudyLab.csproj. For local API\n        testing run dotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100. Open requests.http and use\n        Send Request above each request. Use the actual port from the console if you\n        launch with a different profile.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Your first request</h2>\n    <pre><code>GET http://localhost:5100/api/students</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Initially expect 200 OK with []. A browser address bar can send GET, but cannot\n      conveniently send all CRUD methods. Use the .http editor, Postman, curl.exe or a\n      Swagger UI. The JSON object is a data packet, not an HTML page.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Map each operation to HTTP</h2>\n    <pre><code>POST   /api/students       # Create\nGET    /api/students       # Read all\nGET    /api/students/1     # Read one\nPUT    /api/students/1     # Replace editable data\nDELETE /api/students/1     # Delete</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Expected sequence on a fresh server: GET → 200 []; POST Lefa,22 → 201 with id 1; GET\n      /1 → 200; PUT /1 → 204 with no body; DELETE /1 → 204; GET /1 → 404. IDs can have\n      gaps. In-memory data disappears when the app restarts.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Understand every moving part</h2>\n    <p>\n      MapGet and MapPost register routes. StudentInput is the input DTO; Student is the\n      response record. Results.Created sets 201 and a Location header. Results.NoContent\n      sets 204. StudentStore is registered once for this running app. ConcurrentDictionary\n      and Interlocked make the teaching store safer for simultaneous requests. It is not a\n      distributed database. We validate both POST and PUT, including whitespace-only\n      names.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Send an age of 2. What should happen?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        400 Bad Request. The server must reject invalid input even if a client form also\n        validates it.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Restart and GET all students.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Expect []. In-memory data is lost. The EF examples introduce persistent storage.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-desktop-ado": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    The WPF window collects input. ADO.NET sends SQL to SQL Server and returns the rows to\n    the list on the screen.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prepare and run</h2>\n    <p>\n      This WPF project needs Windows and the .NET desktop development workload. First run\n      setup.sql from project 05-ado-sqlserver in SSMS. Change the local SQL connection if\n      your instance is different, or set STUDYLAB_SQL in the launching environment. Open\n      the desktop project and select ADO.NET SQL Server in the dropdown. Enter Name and\n      Age. Use Create, Read, Update selected and Delete selected.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Trace one button</h2>\n    <p>\n      Create validates the form, opens a SqlConnection, creates an INSERT SqlCommand, adds\n      typed parameters and calls ExecuteNonQuery. Read runs SELECT and loops over\n      SqlDataReader. Selecting a row copies its values into the text boxes. Update/Delete\n      use that selected ID. using closes connections/readers promptly. The simple\n      synchronous desktop handlers are intentionally small; a larger application should\n      use asynchronous I/O to keep the UI responsive.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Expected behaviour</h2>\n    <p>\n      Create Lefa,22 then Read: Lefa appears. Select it, change age to 23, choose Update\n      selected, then Read: age is 23 when selected again. Delete selected removes the row.\n      Invalid input displays a message. SQL errors are shown locally for this lab, not\n      sent to public clients.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Click Update selected with no row selected.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Expected: Select a student. Do not invent an ID from a text box without validating\n        its relationship to the selected record.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Which parts change when moving this repository to an API?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The SQL access rules remain similar. Replace WPF events with HTTP endpoints,\n        scoped services, async methods and safe response/error handling.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-desktop-ef": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    An ORM maps objects to rows. In the desktop lab, choose EF Core SQLite to use a local\n    file database instead of manually writing SQL.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>The building blocks</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Entity: Student, a C# object that represents data. DbSet: Students, the queryable\n        collection for that entity.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        DbContext: the short-lived unit of work that holds configuration and change\n        tracking. Database provider: Sqlite translates supported EF operations to SQLite\n        commands.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        The in-memory model is EF’s description of entity types, keys, properties and\n        relationships. It is not the same thing as the EF InMemory test provider.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Migrations describe schema changes. Change tracking describes entity changes.\n        SaveChanges sends database modifications. Navigation properties describe\n        relationships between entities.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Follow desktop CRUD</h2>\n    <p>\n      The sample creates a fresh SchoolDb for each operation. This avoids leaving a\n      long-lived UI context with stale tracked objects. Add and SaveChanges create rows;\n      Update attaches an edited row; Remove deletes it. AsNoTracking is used for read-only\n      lists. EnsureCreated is used only for this self-contained desktop teaching database;\n      it is not a schema-evolution plan. Use migrations for an evolving real application.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Expected persistence</h2>\n    <p>\n      Create a row, close the window and reopen it, then choose Read. The row remains in\n      the LocalApplicationData database. Deleting the application process does not delete\n      the file. Do not share one DbContext across simultaneous background threads.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Explain model versus database schema.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The model is the application’s mapping description. The schema is the actual\n        database structure; migrations help keep them aligned.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why is a new context per desktop operation helpful?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        It limits tracking lifetime, resource ownership and stale state. A more advanced\n        UI can use IDbContextFactory to create contexts for operations.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-dynamic-assemblies": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    A dynamic assembly is a code container built while the program is running. This is a\n    specialised tool; start with a tiny method that simply returns a number.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Build a method in steps</h2>\n    <p>\n      AssemblyBuilder creates a dynamic assembly; DefineDynamicModule creates its module;\n      DefineType creates a type; DefineMethod declares a method. ILGenerator writes CIL\n      instructions. Ldc_I4_7 pushes the integer7 onto the evaluation stack; Ret returns\n      it. CreateType finishes the generated type so reflection can call it.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Expected result and limits</h2>\n    <p>\n      The full downloadable program emits a public static Seven method and prints Emitted\n      result:7. Dynamic assemblies can support proxies, runtime adapters and generated\n      methods. They are not required for ordinary CRUD and do not automatically compile\n      pasted C# source. Reflection.Emit may be unavailable in restricted/AOT environments.\n      Prefer a normal class when the behaviour is known at build time.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Change the generated method to return 9.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>Use il.Emit(OpCodes.Ldc_I4,9) before Ret, keeping the return type int.</p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why is loading arbitrary generated code dangerous?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        It runs with process permissions. Do not accept untrusted instructions or\n        assemblies as if they were harmless data.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-dynamic-dlr": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    var and dynamic are not the same. var asks the compiler to work out a fixed type.\n    dynamic postpones many member checks until runtime.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try an expandable object</h2>\n    <pre><code>dynamic student = new System.Dynamic.ExpandoObject();\nstudent.Name = &quot;Lefa&quot;;\nConsole.WriteLine(student.Name);</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Expected: Lefa. A misspelled student.Nmae can fail at runtime. With var student =\n      new { Name = \"Lefa\" }, Name is compiler checked and the anonymous object has a fixed\n      read-only shape. dynamic can help with dynamic-object interoperability, but should\n      not replace clear types in normal domain/API code.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>DLR, CLR and intermediate code</h2>\n    <p>\n      C# compiles to Common Intermediate Language and metadata in an assembly. The Common\n      Language Runtime executes managed code, often through JIT compilation; ahead-of-time\n      compilation is another deployment option. The Dynamic Language Runtime supplies\n      dynamic binding infrastructure and call-site caching. It does not turn dynamic into\n      a separate database or make unknown member access safe.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Choose var or dynamic for a normal Student DTO.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Use a named Student type (possibly held in a var local). Keep compile-time checks\n        for routine structured data.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>What does a runtime binder exception suggest?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        A member name, argument list or runtime type did not support the requested\n        operation. Inspect the actual value/type.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-ef-sqlite": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Entity Framework Core is a translator between C# objects and database rows. SQLite\n    stores the database in one file, so you can learn persistence without first setting up\n    a database server.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Install and create the right project</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Install Visual Studio with ASP.NET and web development and a .NET 10-compatible\n        SDK. For WPF also install .NET desktop development. Check Tools → Get Tools and\n        Features if templates are missing.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Create ASP.NET Core Empty for the Minimal API, or ASP.NET Core Web API with Use\n        controllers for a controller project. Select .NET 10. Alternatively open the\n        supplied .csproj to get exactly the lesson files.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Use a separate folder for every example. Delete the generated weather sample if it\n        conflicts with the supplied files. Restore NuGet packages before building.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        In View → Terminal, cd into the folder containing StudyLab.csproj. For local API\n        testing run dotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100. Open requests.http and use\n        Send Request above each request. Use the actual port from the console if you\n        launch with a different profile.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Your first request</h2>\n    <pre><code>GET http://localhost:5100/api/students</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Initially expect 200 OK with []. A browser address bar can send GET, but cannot\n      conveniently send all CRUD methods. Use the .http editor, Postman, curl.exe or a\n      Swagger UI. The JSON object is a data packet, not an HTML page.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Libraries and schema setup</h2>\n    <p>\n      Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite is the database provider.\n      Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design supplies design-time services for migrations.\n      Keep EF packages and dotnet-ef on matching major versions. The project pins package\n      versions in StudyLab.csproj; restore installs them. Built-in System types do not\n      need separate packages.\n    </p>\n    <pre><code>dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef --version 10.0.15\ndotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate\ndotnet ef database update\ndotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      If dotnet-ef is already installed, use dotnet tool update --global dotnet-ef\n      --version 10.0.15 instead. Run migrations inside the project folder. The supplied\n      project has no initial migration until you generate it.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Follow the data</h2>\n    <p>\n      appsettings.json contains ConnectionStrings:School = Data Source=school.db.\n      AddDbContext registers SchoolDb as scoped. DbSet&lt;Student&gt; represents the\n      table. Add marks a new entity; SaveChangesAsync sends the INSERT. FindAsync reads a\n      key; changing a tracked entity and saving sends an UPDATE. Remove and save sends\n      DELETE. AsNoTracking makes read-only lists cheaper. CancellationToken can cancel\n      work when a request is abandoned.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Expected result</h2>\n    <p>\n      Run the same six CRUD requests. Stop and restart the app without deleting school.db;\n      the rows you kept should remain. GET after DELETE still returns 404. A migration\n      changes schema; ordinary CRUD changes rows. EnsureCreated is a convenient separate\n      demo shortcut, but do not mix it into a migrations-managed database.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>You see “no such table: Students”. What is missing?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Run dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate and dotnet ef database update from this\n        project directory before starting the API.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Where is school.db?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The relative connection-string path is resolved from the process working\n        directory. Run from a consistent project folder or configure an absolute path.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-files-json": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    A file is a named box on disk. Serialization turns objects into a format you can save;\n    deserialization turns that saved text back into objects.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Create, read and manipulate files</h2>\n    <pre><code>string folder = Path.Combine(Environment.GetFolderPath(Environment.SpecialFolder.LocalApplicationData), &quot;StudyLabData&quot;);\nDirectory.CreateDirectory(folder);\nstring file = Path.Combine(folder, &quot;notes.txt&quot;);\nFile.WriteAllText(file, &quot;First note&quot;);\nFile.AppendAllText(file, &quot;\\nSecond note&quot;);\nstring[] lines = File.ReadAllLines(file);</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Path.Combine builds a path using the platform’s separators.\n      Directory.CreateDirectory is safe when the directory already exists. ReadAllText\n      reads one string; ReadAllLines reads an array. Use streams for very large files.\n      Check permission and missing-file errors. Do not build arbitrary file paths from\n      untrusted input.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Serialize student objects</h2>\n    <pre><code>File.WriteAllText(file, JsonSerializer.Serialize(students));\nvar loaded = JsonSerializer.Deserialize&lt;List&lt;Student&gt;&gt;(File.ReadAllText(file)) ?? [];</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      System.Text.Json is included with .NET. The desktop sample exports into\n      students.json under LocalApplicationData/StudyLabData. Expected JSON is an array\n      with id, name and age properties (the exact property case depends on options). Read\n      JSON previews the file; it deliberately does not insert those IDs back into the\n      database. Do not use BinaryFormatter for new code.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>What happens when Read JSON is clicked before Export JSON?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The guarded event handler shows a missing-file message. Export first or add a\n        File.Exists check with a friendly prompt.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why does re-importing identity IDs need care?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        They can conflict with existing rows or database identity rules. Validate the\n        import and define an insert/update policy instead of blindly attaching the\n        objects.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-foundations": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <section>\n    <h2>Start with a console student register</h2>\n    <p>\n      You are building a small program that remembers a student name and mark, decides\n      whether the mark is a pass and prints the result. It runs in a text window. Later\n      lessons turn the student idea into an API and a website. Those projects have their\n      own files; do not paste this console Program.cs into an API.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Set up and run</h2>\n    <ol>\n      <li>\n        Install Visual Studio with .NET 10 support. Download this lesson's project and\n        extract it.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Open StudyLab.csproj. Choose Build &gt; Build Solution, then Debug &gt; Start\n        Without Debugging.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        For a terminal, open the folder containing StudyLab.csproj and run\n        <code>dotnet run</code>.\n      </li>\n    </ol>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Understand the pieces</h2>\n    <p>\n      A variable holds a value. A condition chooses which steps run. A loop repeats steps.\n      A method groups steps so you can use them again. A class describes data and\n      behaviour; an object is one instance of that class. A list holds several items.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      Read <code>mark &gt;= 50</code> as \"mark is at least 50\". A single\n      <code>=</code> assigns a value; <code>==</code> compares values. Braces group steps.\n      A comment begins with <code>//</code> and explains the code to a person.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Expected output</h2>\n    <pre><code>Lefa: 65\nPass\nMark: 65\nMark: 40\nMark: 80\nPass\nStudent: Lefa, result: Pass</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      The first decision prints Pass. The loop prints all three list values. The reusable\n      method also returns Pass for 65.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details>\n      <summary>What changes if mark becomes 45?</summary>\n      <p>\n        The first line shows 45. The decision and method results become Try again. The\n        separate list still prints 65, 40 and 80.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details>\n      <summary>How can the list contain a fourth mark?</summary>\n      <p>\n        Call <code>marks.Add(55);</code> before the foreach loop. The loop will print a\n        fourth line: Mark: 55.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-language-tools": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    An operator overload teaches a type what a symbol means. An exception says a job could\n    not be completed. An extension method adds a convenient method-call spelling without\n    changing the original type.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Three tools, three jobs</h2>\n    <pre><code>public readonly record struct Money(decimal Amount)\n{\n    public static Money operator +(Money a, Money b) =&gt; new(a.Amount + b.Amount);\n}\npublic class EnrollmentException(string message) : Exception(message);\npublic static class StudentExtensions\n{\n    public static string Greeting(this string name) =&gt; $&quot;Hello, {name}&quot;;\n}</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Money(10) + Money(5) returns a new Money with Amount 15. The compiler chooses this\n      overload because both operands are Money. Operators should behave predictably. Use\n      exceptions for exceptional failures; use TryParse or validation for ordinary invalid\n      input. Extension methods must be static and declare this on the first parameter;\n      they cannot access another class’s private data.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Expected output and debugging</h2>\n    <pre><code>Money total: 15\nAge cannot be negative.\nHello, lefa</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      The full lab catches EnrollmentException so later examples still run. Catch the most\n      specific useful exception, and use throw; when rethrowing to keep the original stack\n      trace. Never silently swallow an exception.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Add subtraction for Money.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        public static Money operator -(Money a, Money b) =&gt; new(a.Amount - b.Amount);\n        Add it inside Money.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>When should an extension method be avoided?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Avoid hiding important business rules in surprising extensions. A normal service\n        or instance method can make ownership clearer.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-lifetime": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Think of memory as a toy box. The garbage collector can recycle unreachable toys. A\n    file handle is more like a borrowed key: return it promptly, rather than waiting for a\n    cleanup day.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>What the runtime does</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Managed objects live on the managed heap. Roots include active local references,\n        statics and references reachable from them. Unreachable objects can be collected;\n        a value going out of scope does not promise an immediate collection.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Generations 0, 1 and 2 group objects by survival. Collection may move objects and\n        update references. Large objects are handled specially. Do not depend on\n        collection timing for program correctness.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        IDisposable.Dispose releases owned resources such as streams and connections.\n        using is a try/finally shortcut. await using works with IAsyncDisposable. The GC\n        does not call Dispose for you.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        A finalizer is a fallback for unmanaged-resource ownership, not a normal cleanup\n        timer. Prefer SafeHandle over writing a raw finalizer. Avoid GC.Collect in\n        everyday application code.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Read the example</h2>\n    <pre><code>using (var writer = new StreamWriter(&quot;lifetime.txt&quot;))\n{\n    writer.WriteLine(&quot;Saved safely&quot;);\n}\nConsole.WriteLine(File.ReadAllText(&quot;lifetime.txt&quot;).Trim());</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Expected line: <strong>Saved safely</strong>. StreamWriter opens the file; WriteLine\n      writes text; leaving the block closes the writer even if the block throws. System.IO\n      is built into .NET, so no extra NuGet package is needed.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>\n        Why can an unclosed file stay locked even when you no longer use the variable?\n      </summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The reference becoming unreachable does not guarantee immediate finalization.\n        Close your owned stream with using.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Change the code to append two lines.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Use File.AppendAllText(\"lifetime.txt\", \"First\\nSecond\\n\"). ReadAllLines returns\n        one string per line.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-linq": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    LINQ is a set of tools for asking questions about data. Imagine a tray of student\n    cards: keep the passing cards, sort them, then copy only the information you need.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Filter, sort, project and group</h2>\n    <pre><code>var passes = students.Where(s =&gt; s.Mark &gt;= 50)\n    .OrderBy(s =&gt; s.Name)\n    .Select(s =&gt; new { s.Name, s.Mark });\nforeach (var student in passes)\n    Console.WriteLine($&quot;Pass: {student.Name} {student.Mark}&quot;);\nvar average = students.Average(s =&gt; s.Mark);\nvar groups = students.GroupBy(s =&gt; s.Mark &gt;= 50);</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Where filters; OrderBy sorts; Select projects into a small anonymous object.\n      Anonymous types have compiler-generated read-only properties and are useful inside a\n      method. Use named DTOs for API contracts. Expected passing rows: Lefa 80 and Mpho\n      90.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Deferred execution and database queries</h2>\n    <p>\n      Many LINQ operators describe a query without immediately running it. foreach,\n      ToList, Count and similar terminal operations execute it. A List uses\n      LINQ-to-Objects. An EF IQueryable can translate supported operations into SQL. Do\n      filtering and paging before ToListAsync to avoid loading the whole table. First\n      throws when no match exists; FirstOrDefault can return null. Check for null.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Return only names for marks 80 and above.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        students.Where(s =&gt; s.Mark &gt;= 80).Select(s =&gt; s.Name). Expected: Lefa,\n        Mpho.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Explain why ToList before Where can waste memory.</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        It loads every row before filtering. On a database query, filter first and\n        materialize the smaller result.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-mvc-client": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    The website is the shop front. The API is the service counter. They can be separate\n    applications, even when both are written in C#.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Run two applications</h2>\n    <p>\n      Start project 02-controller-api on port 5100 first. Open this MVC project in another\n      Visual Studio window or terminal. Run it on port 5200. Its SchoolApi configuration\n      points to http://localhost:5100/.\n    </p>\n    <pre><code>dotnet run --urls http://localhost:5200</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Open http://localhost:5200 in a browser. Add a student, change the fields and save,\n      then delete the student. The MVC server sends HTTP requests to the API; it does not\n      connect directly to the database.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Understand the files</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Program.cs registers AddControllersWithViews and a named HttpClient.\n        IHttpClientFactory manages client-handler lifetimes.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        The MVC controller calls GetFromJsonAsync, PostAsJsonAsync, PutAsJsonAsync and\n        DeleteAsync. It chooses a Razor View or redirects after a form submission.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Index.cshtml uses @model List&lt;Student&gt; and Razor encoding to display data.\n        Each changing form includes an antiforgery token, and actions use\n        ValidateAntiForgeryToken.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        The browser submits forms to the MVC server. This server-to-server API call does\n        not need CORS. Browser JavaScript calling a different-origin API does need a\n        deliberate CORS policy.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Expected result: a student form and a list of rows, each with Save changes and\n        Delete. An API outage must be handled in a polished app; a connection-refused\n        exception means start/check the API first.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why must the two applications use different ports?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Only one process can listen on the same address and port at once. The client’s\n        configured API base address must match the API’s actual port.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Would allowing every CORS origin secure this API?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. CORS controls which browser origins can read responses. Authentication and\n        authorisation still belong on the server.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-mvvm": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    MVVM keeps the screen separate from the decisions behind it. The View displays; the\n    ViewModel offers screen-friendly properties and commands; the Model holds the\n    underlying data and rules.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Open the Windows example</h2>\n    <p>\n      Open this project on Windows with .NET desktop development installed.\n      MainWindow.xaml is the View. StudentViewModel.cs holds the ViewModel and a reusable\n      command helper. App.xaml chooses the startup window. The window’s DataContext is the\n      object the bindings read. The tiny code-behind only calls InitializeComponent.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Read a binding</h2>\n    <pre><code>&lt;TextBox Text=&quot;{Binding Name, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged}&quot;/&gt;\n&lt;Button Content=&quot;Add student&quot; Command=&quot;{Binding AddCommand}&quot;/&gt;</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Binding Name reads and writes the ViewModel’s Name property. PropertyChanged updates\n      the source while typing. A command expresses an action without putting all business\n      logic in a click handler. ObservableCollection tells the View when items are added\n      or removed. Expected: Add remains disabled until a valid name and age are entered;\n      after adding, a new list entry appears.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Keep layers understandable</h2>\n    <p>\n      Use services/repositories for database work rather than building SQL strings in\n      XAML. The Model is not always an EF entity: it can be a domain object or DTO. MVVM\n      and MVC solve related separation problems for different UI styles; their third parts\n      have different jobs.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Where should a “Can enrol?” rule live?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        In a model/domain service, used by the ViewModel. Avoid burying reusable rules\n        only in a UI control.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>What happens if DataContext is missing?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Bindings may have no source. Inspect the Visual Studio Output window for binding\n        errors; set the intended ViewModel.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-notifications-validation": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Changing a C# field does not automatically tell the screen. Notifications are the\n    message saying “this property changed; please redraw it.”\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>INotifyPropertyChanged</h2>\n    <pre><code>public event PropertyChangedEventHandler? PropertyChanged;\nvoid Changed([CallerMemberName] string? property = null) =&gt;\n    PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(property));</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      The property setter updates its backing field and raises the event with the property\n      name. CallerMemberName avoids repeatedly typing the name. A wrong name can stop the\n      expected UI refresh. ObservableCollection handles collection changes, but an item’s\n      changing properties still need their own notifications.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Validate without crashing</h2>\n    <p>\n      The sample implements IDataErrorInfo. Its indexer returns a message for Name or\n      AgeText. XAML sets ValidatesOnDataErrors=True. AgeText is a string so incomplete\n      typing does not cause repeated conversion exceptions; int.TryParse checks it. Red\n      validation feedback and a disabled command guide the user. Server-side validation is\n      still required if the ViewModel later calls an API.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Expected checks</h2>\n    <p>\n      Blank name: invalid. Name over 80 characters: invalid. Age abc,15 or101: invalid. A\n      normal name and age22 enable Add. After Add, the list changes and inputs reset. A\n      larger app can use INotifyDataErrorInfo for asynchronous validation.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why does changing a field directly not refresh a bound TextBlock?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>No PropertyChanged notification was raised. Set through a notifying property.</p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Does desktop validation replace API validation?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. Any client can send requests. The server independently enforces its rules.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-openapi": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    OpenAPI is a machine-readable map of your API. Swagger UI draws a test page from that\n    map. They are separate tools.\n  </p>\n  <h2>Open and run the complete example</h2>\n  <p>\n    Download the ZIP below and open StudyLab.csproj in Visual Studio. Restore its\n    packages. The project includes Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi for generating the\n    document and Swashbuckle.AspNetCore.SwaggerUI for displaying it.\n  </p>\n  <p>In Visual Studio’s PowerShell terminal, inside the project folder:</p>\n  <pre><code>$env:ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=\"Development\"\ndotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100</code></pre>\n  <p>\n    Open http://localhost:5100/openapi/v1.json to see the document. Open\n    http://localhost:5100/swagger to see the test UI. Expand POST /api/students, choose\n    Try it out, paste {\"name\":\"Lefa\",\"age\":22}, then Execute. Expected: 201 Created. Use\n    GET to read it, PUT to edit, DELETE to remove it, then GET again for404.\n  </p>\n  <h2>Read Program.cs</h2>\n  <p>\n    AddOpenApi registers the generator. MapOpenApi exposes the document route.\n    UseSwaggerUI points the viewer at that route. MapControllers maps the actual API\n    operations. This combination does not need AddSwaggerGen. If /swagger gives404, check\n    the environment, installed UI package, port and Program.cs; the built-in .NET10\n    template alone does not promise a Swagger UI.\n  </p>\n  <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n  <details class=\"panel\">\n    <summary>\n      Why should the document and UI routes be restricted in a deployed service?\n    </summary>\n    <p>\n      They can reveal the API surface and permit convenient requests. Expose them only\n      deliberately, with suitable access control. Hiding documentation is not a substitute\n      for securing endpoints.\n    </p>\n  </details>\n  <details class=\"panel\">\n    <summary>What should happen when you post an age of2?</summary>\n    <p>\n      The controller’s validation should return400. An attractive UI does not replace\n      server-side validation.\n    </p>\n  </details>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-processes": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    A process is a running program with its own operating-system resources. A thread is a\n    worker inside that process. These ideas are related, but they are not interchangeable.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Modern .NET and older textbook terminology</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        System.Diagnostics.Process inspects processes and can start other programs.\n        Dispose Process objects you create. Do not start arbitrary commands from untrusted\n        input.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        The CLR runs managed code and manages type safety, exceptions and garbage\n        collection. AppDomain.CurrentDomain is still available for information. Creating\n        and unloading secondary AppDomains is a .NET Framework technique, not a modern\n        .NET isolation recipe.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Modern .NET uses AssemblyLoadContext for loading assemblies. A collectible context\n        can support unloadable plugins but is not a security sandbox.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Object contexts in older .NET Framework remoting describe groups of context-bound\n        objects with shared services. ContextBoundObject and remoting are legacy subjects.\n        In ASP.NET Core, an HTTP context and an EF DbContext are different concepts.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it</h2>\n    <pre><code>using var process = Process.GetCurrentProcess();\nConsole.WriteLine(process.ProcessName);\nConsole.WriteLine(AppDomain.CurrentDomain.FriendlyName);</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Expected output includes the executable or host name. It varies by machine and\n      launch method. Do not memorise a fixed process ID.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Is an EF DbContext a separate process?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. It is an object that tracks database work inside your application process.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>What should isolate an untrusted plugin?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        A separately secured process or container boundary, with carefully controlled\n        permissions. AssemblyLoadContext alone is not a security boundary.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-reflection": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Reflection lets a running program inspect a type’s description. It is like reading\n    labels on a toolbox before choosing a tool.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Inspect and invoke</h2>\n    <pre><code>Type type = typeof(MarkRules);\nvar method = type.GetMethod(nameof(MarkRules.IsPass))!;\nobject? result = method.Invoke(null, new object[] { 40 });</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Expected late-binding result: False. null is the target for a static method.\n      Parameters must match the signature. For an instance method create/provide an\n      instance. Check for a missing method instead of using null-forgiving syntax in\n      general dynamic input code. Invocation errors can wrap the inner failure in\n      TargetInvocationException.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Attributes are metadata</h2>\n    <p>\n      StudyTopicAttribute attaches a Title to MarkRules. AttributeUsage limits where it\n      can appear. GetCustomAttribute reads it; an attribute does not execute a rule by\n      magic. Reflection is useful for serializers, discovery, testing tools and plugin\n      contracts, but ordinary direct calls are clearer and checked earlier. Trimming and\n      Native AOT can require explicit metadata preservation; arbitrary reflection is not\n      always deployment-friendly.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>What changes if IsPass becomes an instance method?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>Create an instance and pass it as Invoke’s target instead of null.</p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>How do you handle a user-supplied method name?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Avoid unrestricted invocation. Allowlist approved operations and validate\n        arguments and access. Reflection is not an authorisation system.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-sqlserver": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    SQL Server is a separate database service. Your API needs the server address, database\n    name and a permitted identity, just like needing an address and a key for a cupboard.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Install and create the right project</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Install Visual Studio with ASP.NET and web development and a .NET 10-compatible\n        SDK. For WPF also install .NET desktop development. Check Tools → Get Tools and\n        Features if templates are missing.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Create ASP.NET Core Empty for the Minimal API, or ASP.NET Core Web API with Use\n        controllers for a controller project. Select .NET 10. Alternatively open the\n        supplied .csproj to get exactly the lesson files.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Use a separate folder for every example. Delete the generated weather sample if it\n        conflicts with the supplied files. Restore NuGet packages before building.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        In View → Terminal, cd into the folder containing StudyLab.csproj. For local API\n        testing run dotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100. Open requests.http and use\n        Send Request above each request. Use the actual port from the console if you\n        launch with a different profile.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Your first request</h2>\n    <pre><code>GET http://localhost:5100/api/students</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Initially expect 200 OK with []. A browser address bar can send GET, but cannot\n      conveniently send all CRUD methods. Use the .http editor, Postman, curl.exe or a\n      Swagger UI. The JSON object is a data packet, not an HTML page.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Windows Authentication in your lab</h2>\n    <pre><code>Server=LEFA\\SQLEXPRESS;Database=StudyLabSchool;Trusted_Connection=True;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Use your actual instance name. In appsettings.json a backslash must be escaped, so\n      the file contains LEFA\\\\SQLEXPRESS. Trusted_Connection uses the Windows account\n      running the API, not automatically the person using a website. Install the SQL\n      Server provider and Design package already declared in this project. Ensure the SQL\n      Server service is running and you can connect in SSMS.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Create and update schema</h2>\n    <pre><code>dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef --version 10.0.15\ndotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate\ndotnet ef database update\ndotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Use tool update if already installed. The migration creates the database/table when\n      your account has permission. TrustServerCertificate=True is only a local teaching\n      shortcut for an untrusted development certificate. For a real deployment use a valid\n      certificate and validate it. SQL Server schemas and SQLite schemas should use\n      separate provider-specific migration folders/projects.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Secrets and connection troubleshooting</h2>\n    <pre><code>dotnet user-secrets init\ndotnet user-secrets set &quot;ConnectionStrings:School&quot; &quot;Server=YOUR_SERVER;Database=StudyLabSchool;User Id=YOUR_LOGIN;Password=YOUR_LOCAL_PASSWORD;Encrypt=True;TrustServerCertificate=True&quot;</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Run secret commands only with your own local values. Do not paste real passwords\n      into screenshots, source control or example requests. User secrets are a development\n      convenience, not an encrypted production vault. Login failed: check the identity and\n      permissions. Server not found: check instance, TCP configuration and service.\n      Certificate error: fix trust/certificate configuration. A connection string does not\n      install SQL Server.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why does Windows Authentication fail inside a Linux container?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        A Linux container does not automatically carry your interactive Windows login. The\n        Docker exercise uses an explicit database credential through environment\n        configuration.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>How does ConnectionStrings__School work?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Double underscore represents the nested configuration separator in an environment\n        variable. It overrides ConnectionStrings:School for the running process.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-threads": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    One worker can do jobs in order. Several workers can work at the same time, but they\n    must not fight over the same notebook.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Make shared updates safe</h2>\n    <pre><code>int count = 0;\nParallel.For(0, 1000, _ =&gt; Interlocked.Increment(ref count));\nConsole.WriteLine($&quot;Safe count: {count}&quot;);\nvar worker = new Thread(() =&gt; Console.WriteLine(&quot;Worker thread finished&quot;));\nworker.Start();\nworker.Join();</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Expected count: 1000. count++ reads, adds and writes, so racing workers can lose\n      updates. Interlocked makes this small update atomic. lock protects a larger critical\n      section; keep locks short. Join waits for a thread to finish and blocks the caller.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Choose the right model</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Multithreading: more than one thread can make progress.</li>\n      <li>\n        Parallelism: work executes at the same time, useful for sufficiently large CPU\n        calculations. Task.Run can offload CPU work from a desktop UI thread.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Asynchrony: do not block while waiting for I/O. It can use the same thread again\n        later; async does not always create a new thread.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        WPF controls belong to their UI thread. Use Dispatcher for UI updates from another\n        thread. Never share a DbContext across parallel tasks; give independent work\n        separate scopes or contexts.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why is a static List dangerous for simultaneous API writes?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Concurrent mutation can corrupt assumptions. Use a real database, a properly\n        locked store, or a suitable concurrent collection for an in-memory demo.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>When is Parallel.For a bad choice?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        For tiny jobs or waiting on network I/O, overhead or blocked workers can make it\n        slower.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/cs-web-foundations": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    ASP.NET Core is the toolbox for building a web server in C#. The server waits for HTTP\n    requests, then runs a matching endpoint. Start slowly: create the project, build it,\n    and send one request.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Install and create the right project</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        Install Visual Studio with ASP.NET and web development and a .NET 10-compatible\n        SDK. For WPF also install .NET desktop development. Check Tools → Get Tools and\n        Features if templates are missing.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Create ASP.NET Core Empty for the Minimal API, or ASP.NET Core Web API with Use\n        controllers for a controller project. Select .NET 10. Alternatively open the\n        supplied .csproj to get exactly the lesson files.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Use a separate folder for every example. Delete the generated weather sample if it\n        conflicts with the supplied files. Restore NuGet packages before building.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        In View → Terminal, cd into the folder containing StudyLab.csproj. For local API\n        testing run dotnet run --urls http://localhost:5100. Open requests.http and use\n        Send Request above each request. Use the actual port from the console if you\n        launch with a different profile.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Your first request</h2>\n    <pre><code>GET http://localhost:5100/api/students</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Initially expect 200 OK with []. A browser address bar can send GET, but cannot\n      conveniently send all CRUD methods. Use the .http editor, Postman, curl.exe or a\n      Swagger UI. The JSON object is a data packet, not an HTML page.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>MVC in simple words</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Model: the data and rules, such as Student and valid ages.</li>\n      <li>\n        View: the HTML the person sees. A Razor .cshtml view creates HTML on the server.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        Controller: accepts input, calls application/data services and chooses a response.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        A Web API controller normally returns JSON, not a Razor view. ControllerBase is\n        for APIs; Controller adds view support. REST is a style of resource-oriented HTTP\n        design, not a specific NuGet package.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>What ASP.NET Core gives you</h2>\n    <p>\n      Program.cs builds the host. The dependency-injection container creates shared\n      services. Middleware runs in order around requests. Routing selects an endpoint.\n      Configuration reads appsettings.json, environment variables and development user\n      secrets. Logging records events. Kestrel serves HTTP. AddControllers registers\n      controller services; MapControllers connects attributed routes to the endpoint\n      pipeline.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Is a JSON API response a View?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. A Razor View is server-rendered HTML. JSON is data that a website, mobile app\n        or other client can use.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>\n        What does 404 mean if GET /api/students succeeds but GET /students fails?\n      </summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The server is running, but /students is not the route. Use the exact route\n        declared by the controller.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/csharp-beginner-guide": "<section class=\"panel\" aria-labelledby=\"beginner-start\">\n  <h2 id=\"beginner-start\">New to C#? Start here</h2>\n  <p>{{dynamic_1}}</p>\n  <p>\n    C# is the language you write. .NET provides tools and ready-made features that run\n    your program. Visual Studio is the app where you edit, build and run it.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    Start with <a href=\"#cs-lesson/cs-foundations\">C# foundations</a>. Then follow the web\n    learning order: <a href=\"#cs-lesson/cs-web-foundations\">1. Understand web apps</a>,\n    <a href=\"#cs-lesson/cs-crud-minimal\">2. Try beginner CRUD</a>,\n    <a href=\"#cs-lesson/cs-crud-controllers\">3. Organise code with controllers</a>, then\n    <a href=\"#cs-lesson/cs-ef-sqlite\">4. Save data in a database</a>. You can return to\n    the other units whenever you need them.\n  </p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>Read the short explanation first. You do not need to memorise every word.</li>\n    <li>\n      Download the example ZIP, extract it, then open its .csproj file in Visual Studio.\n      This file tells Visual Studio how to build the project.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      Wait for package restore. A package is a library of ready-made code. NuGet downloads\n      the packages listed in the project.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      Follow one numbered part at a time. Add the parts to the same named file, in order.\n      The full version is below if you get stuck.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      Choose Build &gt; Build Solution to check the code. Then follow this lesson's run\n      instructions and compare the result with its expected output.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      Change one small thing, run again and explain what changed. Try the task before\n      reading its answer.\n    </li>\n  </ol>\n  <details>\n    <summary>Try your first C# program in a separate Console App</summary>\n    <p>\n      In Visual Studio choose Create a new project &gt; Console App (C#), then select .NET\n      10. Replace everything in Program.cs with this code. Keep this practice project\n      separate from the API and desktop projects.\n    </p>\n    <div class=\"cs-source-file\">\n      <pre tabindex=\"0\"><code><span class=\"code-comment\">// Store a name as text.</span>\n<span class=\"code-keyword\">string</span> name = <span class=\"code-string\">\"Lefa\"</span>;\n<span class=\"code-comment\">// Store an age as a whole number.</span>\n<span class=\"code-keyword\">int</span> age = <span class=\"code-number\">22</span>;\n<span class=\"code-comment\">// Print a message. Braces insert the stored values.</span>\n<span class=\"code-type\">Console</span>.WriteLine($<span class=\"code-string\">\"Hello {name}. You are {age}.\"</span>);</code></pre>\n    </div>\n    <p>\n      Choose Debug &gt; Start Without Debugging. Expected output:\n      <samp>Hello Lefa. You are 22.</samp>\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      <code>string</code> means text. <code>int</code> means a whole number. A variable is\n      a named place to store a value. <code>=</code> stores the value on the right in the\n      variable on the left. Double quotes surround text. A semicolon ends these\n      statements. <code>//</code> starts a comment for the reader; the program ignores it.\n    </p>\n    <p>\n      Try it: change the name to your own and add 1 to the age. Predict the message, then\n      run it again.\n    </p>\n  </details>\n  <details>\n    <summary>Simple meanings of words used in these lessons</summary>\n    <dl>\n      <dt>Class and object</dt>\n      <dd>\n        A class describes a kind of thing. An object is one thing created from that\n        description. A Student class can describe many student objects.\n      </dd>\n      <dt>Property</dt>\n      <dd>A named value on an object, such as a student's Name or Age.</dd>\n      <dt>Method, parameter and return</dt>\n      <dd>\n        A method is a named set of steps. A parameter receives input. Return sends a\n        result back to the code that called it.\n      </dd>\n      <dt>List and LINQ</dt>\n      <dd>\n        A list holds several items. LINQ lets you find, sort or select information from\n        those items.\n      </dd>\n      <dt>API, request and response</dt>\n      <dd>\n        An API lets programs ask each other to do work. A request is the question sent to\n        it. A response is the result sent back.\n      </dd>\n      <dt>Route and endpoint</dt>\n      <dd>\n        A route is an address pattern such as /api/students. An endpoint connects an\n        address and HTTP action to the code that handles it.\n      </dd>\n      <dt>CRUD</dt>\n      <dd>Create adds an item. Read shows it. Update changes it. Delete removes it.</dd>\n      <dt>DTO and JSON</dt>\n      <dd>\n        A DTO is a small object used to carry data into or out of an API. JSON is a text\n        format used to send that data.\n      </dd>\n      <dt>Database, EF Core and migration</dt>\n      <dd>\n        A database stores rows that remain after the app closes. EF Core helps C# work\n        with those rows. A migration describes a change to database tables.\n      </dd>\n      <dt>Service and dependency injection</dt>\n      <dd>\n        A service does a job for other code. Dependency injection means .NET supplies a\n        registered service when your code needs it.\n      </dd>\n      <dt>async and await</dt>\n      <dd>\n        Some work, such as reading a database, takes time. Await lets a method wait for\n        that work without keeping its thread busy. Async allows await in the method. It\n        does not automatically create a new thread.\n      </dd>\n      <dt>Build error and runtime error</dt>\n      <dd>\n        A build error stops the code from compiling. A runtime error happens after the\n        program starts. Read the first error, check its file and line, then fix one issue\n        at a time.\n      </dd>\n    </dl>\n  </details>\n</section>\n",
  "studylab/csharp-course-shell": "<p class=\"eyebrow\">{{dynamic_1}}</p>\n<h1>{{dynamic_2}}</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">{{dynamic_3}}</p>\n<div class=\"button-row\">{{dynamic_4}}</div>\n<div id=\"csharp-course-content\">{{dynamic_5}}</div>\n",
  "studylab/csharp-lesson-shell": "<div class=\"cs-lesson-layout\">\n  <p class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"{{dynamic_1}}\">{{dynamic_2}}</a> / {{dynamic_3}}</p>\n  <h1>{{dynamic_4}}</h1>\n  <p class=\"lede\">{{dynamic_5}}</p>\n  <div class=\"note\">\n    Learn here, then test in Visual Studio. StudyLab displays C# and Docker examples; it\n    does not execute C# or containers in this browser.\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <button type=\"button\" class=\"button secondary\" id=\"cs-jump-code\">\n      View the coded example\n    </button>\n  </div>\n  {{dynamic_12}} {{dynamic_13}} {{dynamic_11}} {{dynamic_6}}\n  <section class=\"panel\">\n    <h2 id=\"cs-code-title\" tabindex=\"-1\" style=\"scroll-margin-top: 96px\">\n      Build the code part by part\n    </h2>\n    <p>\n      Work through the files in the suggested order. Each numbered part includes comments\n      explaining its job. Fragments must be combined in the same file before building; do\n      not create a separate project for every part.\n    </p>\n    <p id=\"cs-parts-status\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n    {{dynamic_10}}\n  </section>\n  <section class=\"panel\" id=\"cs-complete-code\">\n    <h2>Overall code . All project files together</h2>\n    <p>\n      The code below is the same code supplied in the downloadable project. Read it here,\n      copy one file at a time, or download the complete ZIP. Paste each file into that\n      exact file in Visual Studio. Keep examples in separate folders. Replace existing\n      file contents instead of pasting a second Program.cs underneath the first.\n    </p>\n    <button class=\"button secondary\" type=\"button\" id=\"cs-download-project\">\n      Download complete project ZIP\n    </button>\n    <p id=\"cs-download-status\" role=\"status\"></p>\n    <div class=\"button-row\">\n      <button type=\"button\" class=\"button secondary\" id=\"cs-expand-code\">\n        Show all code files</button\n      ><button type=\"button\" class=\"button secondary\" id=\"cs-collapse-code\">\n        Collapse code files\n      </button>\n    </div>\n    <p id=\"cs-copy-status\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n    {{dynamic_7}}\n  </section>\n  {{dynamic_14}}\n  <section class=\"panel\" aria-labelledby=\"cs-quiz-heading\">\n    <h2 id=\"cs-quiz-heading\">Check your understanding</h2>\n    <div id=\"cs-quiz\">{{dynamic_8}}</div>\n    <p id=\"cs-quiz-status\" role=\"status\"></p>\n    <button type=\"button\" class=\"button\" id=\"cs-check-quiz\">Check answers</button>\n  </section>\n  <section class=\"panel\">\n    <h2>Your progress</h2>\n    <label\n      ><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"cs-complete\" /> I have read the lesson and tried its\n      practical work.</label\n    >\n    <p id=\"cs-progress-status\" role=\"status\"></p>\n  </section>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">{{dynamic_9}}</div>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/csharp-project-brief": "<section class=\"panel project-brief\" aria-labelledby=\"project-brief-heading\">\n  <p class=\"eyebrow\">Before you write code</p>\n  <h2 id=\"project-brief-heading\">What you are building</h2>\n  <h3>{{dynamic_1}}</h3>\n  <p>{{dynamic_2}}</p>\n  <h3>In this lesson</h3>\n  <p>{{dynamic_3}}</p>\n  <h3>Where the data goes</h3>\n  <p>{{dynamic_4}}</p>\n  <h3>What to try when it works</h3>\n  <p>{{dynamic_5}}</p>\n</section>\n",
  "studylab/database-downloads": "<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>New database revision pilot</h2>\n  <p>\n    Try the free five-page sample with SQL questions and 1NF to 3NF dependency diagrams.\n    The complete pack is in review and is not yet for sale.\n  </p>\n  <a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"/learn/study-packs/\"\n    >Preview study packs and download the free sample</a\n  >\n</section>\n<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by databaseResources(). -->\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">DATABASES . RESOURCES</p>\n<h1>Take your practice further.</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Download native demo setup scripts from the SQL workspace, or print a unit overview or\n  lesson as PDF.\n</p>\n<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Database study packs</h2>\n  <p>\n    Revision selections with matching question numbers. The full exercise bank remains\n    online.\n  </p>\n  {{dynamic_1}}\n</section>\n<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Native SQL Server verification</h2>\n  <p>\n    Native execution is pending. This build environment has no SQL Server instance. The\n    validation harness checks constraints, referential actions, transactions, OUTPUT,\n    indexes, views, procedures and runtime error handling when you run it in a disposable\n    SQL Server database.\n  </p>\n  <a\n    class=\"button secondary\"\n    href=\"./downloads/databases/sqlserver-validation.sql\"\n    download\n    >Download native validation script</a\n  >\n  <p>\n    Create and select a disposable user database named StudyLabNativeValidation in SSMS.\n    Review the script, run it, and keep its PASS output. It rejects other database\n    contexts and rolls back its fixture objects. This harness covers representative\n    behaviours; it does not certify every lesson example.\n  </p>\n</section>\n<div class=\"hub-grid\">\n  <article class=\"panel hub-card\">\n    <h2>Demo data and SQL scripts</h2>\n    <p>\n      Select Microsoft SQL Server, PostgreSQL or MySQL, then download the setup script and\n      your current query.\n    </p>\n    <a class=\"button\" href=\"#sql\">Open SQL downloads</a>\n  </article>\n  <article class=\"panel hub-card\">\n    <h2>Unit overview sheets</h2>\n    <p>\n      Each overview includes outcomes, themes, study guidance and a printable summary.\n    </p>\n    <a class=\"button\" href=\"#db\">Choose a learning unit</a>\n  </article>\n</div>\n<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>DATA6222 Intermediate examples</h2>\n  <p>\n    In SSMS, select a disposable user database and run the college setup first. Each\n    lesson labels its environment and prerequisites. Design notation downloads as text.\n    Native scripts have been reviewed but have not been executed on a SQL Server instance\n    here.\n  </p>\n  <a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"./downloads/intermediate/college-setup.sql\" download\n    >Download college setup for SQL Server</a\n  >{{dynamic_2}}\n</section>\n<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Advanced Database examples</h2>\n  <p>\n    Eleven lesson examples covering advanced databases plus Hadoop, HDFS, YARN, MapReduce\n    and Hive. SQL Server examples are labelled for SSMS. Hadoop shell commands, MapReduce\n    pseudocode, HiveQL and design notes download as text for their separate environments.\n    Native execution remains pending.\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    {{dynamic_3}}\n  </ul>\n  <a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#db-advanced\">Explore Advanced Database</a>\n</section>\n<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Official reference documentation</h2>\n  <ul>\n    <li>\n      <a\n        class=\"text-link\"\n        href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-transact-sql/\"\n        target=\"_blank\"\n        rel=\"noopener\"\n        >Microsoft SQL Server SELECT reference</a\n      >\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      <a\n        class=\"text-link\"\n        href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-select.html\"\n        target=\"_blank\"\n        rel=\"noopener\"\n        >PostgreSQL SELECT reference</a\n      >\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      <a\n        class=\"text-link\"\n        href=\"https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/select.html\"\n        target=\"_blank\"\n        rel=\"noopener\"\n        >MySQL SELECT reference</a\n      >\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      <a class=\"text-link\" href=\"https://sql.js.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"\n        >sql.js: the local SQLite engine used in this workspace</a\n      >\n    </li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"muted\">\n    Database question sheets, model answers and revision PowerPoints are available above.\n    PRLD5121 downloads remain in the PRLD5121 module.\n  </p>\n</section>\n",
  "studylab/database-home": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by databaseHome(). -->\n<section class=\"welcome\">\n  <p class=\"eyebrow\">{{dynamic_1}}</p>\n  <h1>Understand data. Build better databases.</h1>\n  <p class=\"lede\">\n    Start with the ideas, design the relationships, then put SQL into practice. Choose a\n    learning unit below.\n  </p>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <a class=\"button\" href=\"#db-unit/{{dynamic_2}}\">Start with Unit 1</a\n    ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#sql\">Open SQL workspace</a>\n  </div>\n</section>\n{{dynamic_3}}\n<div class=\"section-heading\">\n  <h2>Explore the database course</h2>\n  <p>{{dynamic_4}}</p>\n</div>\n{{dynamic_5}}\n<div class=\"button-row\">\n  <a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#db\">Introduction . DATA6211</a\n  ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#db-intermediate\">Intermediate . DATA6222</a\n  ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#db-advanced\">Advanced Database</a>\n</div>\n<p class=\"note\">\n  Original supplementary material aligned to the supplied course outlines. Textbook\n  chapter numbers follow that outline; these lessons do not reproduce the prescribed\n  textbook.\n</p>\n",
  "studylab/database-lesson": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by databaseLesson(). -->\n<p class=\"breadcrumb\">\n  <a href=\"#{{dynamic_1}}\">Databases</a><span>/</span\n  ><a href=\"#db-unit/{{dynamic_2}}\">Unit {{dynamic_3}} overview</a>\n</p>\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">{{dynamic_4}} . UNIT {{dynamic_5}}</p>\n<h1>{{dynamic_6}}</h1>\n<div class=\"lesson-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Database lesson sections\">\n  {{dynamic_7}}\n</div>\n<section id=\"db-panel\" class=\"panel\" role=\"tabpanel\"></section>\n<nav class=\"lesson-paging\" aria-label=\"Lesson navigation\">{{dynamic_8}}{{dynamic_9}}</nav>\n<button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"db-print\">Print / save this view as PDF</button>\n",
  "studylab/database-practice": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by databasePractice(). -->\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">DATABASES . PRACTICE</p>\n<h1>Choose what to practise.</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Use lesson quizzes for quick checks, written tasks for design reasoning, and the SQL\n  workspace for executable queries.\n</p>\n<div class=\"hub-grid\">\n  <article class=\"panel hub-card\">\n    <h2>Database practice exams</h2>\n    <p>\n      Mixed-unit quiz questions, two written tasks, optional timers and worked solutions\n      after submission.\n    </p>\n    <div class=\"button-row\">\n      <a class=\"button\" href=\"#db-exams/database\">DATA6211 exam</a\n      ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#db-exams/intermediate\">DATA6222 exam</a>\n    </div>\n  </article>\n  <article class=\"panel hub-card\">\n    <h2>Write and run SQL</h2>\n    <p>\n      Seven fictional datasets, three dialect choices, query examples and practical\n      challenges.\n    </p>\n    <a class=\"button\" href=\"#sql\">Open SQL workspace</a>\n  </article>\n  <article class=\"panel hub-card\">\n    <h2>Normalisation diagrams</h2>\n    <p>\n      Nine attribute-row diagram cases, 54 questions, 1NF to 3NF rules and worked\n      decompositions.\n    </p>\n    <a class=\"button\" href=\"#nf-builder/projects/standard\">Build and check diagrams</a\n    ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#normalisation/projects\"\n      >Rules and diagram questions</a\n    >\n  </article>\n</div>\n<h2>Quizzes and written tasks by unit</h2>\n<div class=\"course-lesson-links\">{{dynamic_1}}</div>\n<p class=\"note\">\n  Each database lesson has a Quiz and a Try it yourself tab. Written model answers unlock\n  after you submit an attempt.\n</p>\n",
  "studylab/database-unit": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by databaseUnit(). -->\n<p class=\"breadcrumb\">\n  <a href=\"#{{dynamic_1}}\">Databases</a><span>/</span\n  ><strong>Unit {{dynamic_2}} overview</strong>\n</p>\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">{{dynamic_3}} . LEARNING UNIT {{dynamic_4}}</p>\n<h1>{{dynamic_5}}</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">{{dynamic_6}}</p>\n<section class=\"panel unit-overview-panel\">\n  <h2>What you will learn</h2>\n  <ol>\n    {{dynamic_7}}\n  </ol>\n  <h3>Themes in this unit</h3>\n  <ul>\n    {{dynamic_8}}\n  </ul>\n  <p>{{dynamic_9}} {{dynamic_10}}</p>\n  <h3>How to study this unit</h3>\n  <p>\n    Read each lesson and work through its example. Attempt the quiz and written task\n    before revealing the explanation. {{dynamic_11}}\n  </p>\n  <div class=\"note\"><strong>Before moving on:</strong> {{dynamic_12}}</div>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <a class=\"button\" href=\"#db-lesson/{{dynamic_13}}\">Start this unit</a\n    >{{dynamic_14}}<button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"print-db-unit\">\n      Print / save overview as PDF\n    </button>\n  </div>\n</section>\n<div class=\"section-heading\"><h2>Lessons in this unit</h2></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-list\">{{dynamic_15}}</div>\n",
  "studylab/docker-cli": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Build makes the package; run starts it. Inspection and logs help you understand what\n    is happening instead of guessing.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prepare your computer</h2>\n    <p>\n      Install Docker Desktop with Linux containers enabled and the .NET10 SDK. For the\n      full SQL Server stack use supported x64 hardware with enough memory for SQL Server.\n      WPF does not run in the Linux ASP.NET image. Accept applicable vendor licences\n      yourself during installation. Run docker version and docker compose version in a\n      terminal; start Docker Desktop if the engine cannot be reached.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try the standalone image</h2>\n    <pre><code>docker build -t studylab-api:1 ./Api\ndocker run --name school-api -d -p 127.0.0.1:5100:8080 studylab-api:1\ndocker logs school-api\ndocker inspect school-api\ndocker stop school-api\ndocker start school-api\ndocker rm -f school-api</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      With no database configuration the standalone image can answer /health, but CRUD\n      needs the database connection supplied by Compose. The health route proves the API\n      process can answer, not that every dependency is healthy. rm removes only this\n      practice container; it does not uninstall Docker.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Registry workflow</h2>\n    <pre><code>docker tag studylab-api:1 YOUR_ACCOUNT/studylab-api:1\ndocker login\ndocker push YOUR_ACCOUNT/studylab-api:1\ndocker pull YOUR_ACCOUNT/studylab-api:1</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Use your own registry account and approved access. Do not publish images containing\n      secrets or private student data. Use immutable version tags/digests for reproducible\n      deployment, and scan/update base images. A later pull may differ if a mutable tag\n      was replaced.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Port5100 is already allocated. What next?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Stop the earlier local API/container or use a different host port, then update the\n        client URL.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>What is the difference between stop and rm?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        stop ends the process but keeps the container definition/writable layer. rm\n        deletes the container.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/docker-compose-stack": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Compose is a set of instructions for several containers that belong together. Our\n    stack has a website, an API and SQL Server.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prepare your computer</h2>\n    <p>\n      Install Docker Desktop with Linux containers enabled and the .NET10 SDK. For the\n      full SQL Server stack use supported x64 hardware with enough memory for SQL Server.\n      WPF does not run in the Linux ASP.NET image. Accept applicable vendor licences\n      yourself during installation. Run docker version and docker compose version in a\n      terminal; start Docker Desktop if the engine cannot be reached.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Before the first start</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the complete stack. It contains Api, Website, compose.yaml and\n      .env.example. Copy .env.example to .env in the root folder. Replace the\n      demonstration password with your own strong local password. Do not commit .env. In\n      Api generate InitialCreate with dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate before\n      building the images. The classroom API applies those migrations when\n      StudyLabMigrate=true.\n    </p>\n    <pre><code>docker compose config --quiet\ndocker compose up --build -d\ndocker compose ps\ndocker compose logs api</code></pre>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Follow the startup order</h2>\n    <p>\n      db starts SQL Server Developer edition and stores files in school-data. Its sqlcmd\n      healthcheck must succeed before api starts. api retries transient database\n      connections and performs its classroom migration. website starts after the API\n      container is started; that is not the same as API readiness, so wait for the API\n      migration to finish in logs before opening the website.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Test every tier</h2>\n    <p>\n      Open http://localhost:5100/health, then http://localhost:5100/api/students, then\n      http://localhost:5200. Expected: a ready health response, an empty list on a new\n      database, then an MVC form. Create Lefa,22 in the website. The website calls api;\n      api writes a SQL Server row. Read, update and delete through the form. Keep a row\n      for the volume test.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why use db,1433 instead of localhost inside api?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        localhost inside api means the API container itself. Compose DNS resolves db to\n        the database service.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>\n        Is depends_on:service_started enough to prove SQL Server can accept connections?\n      </summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. The database uses a healthcheck and service_healthy condition, plus transient\n        retry handling.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/docker-containers": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    An image is a recipe packed with application files. A container is a running copy of\n    that recipe. A volume is a storage box kept outside that disposable running copy.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prepare your computer</h2>\n    <p>\n      Install Docker Desktop with Linux containers enabled and the .NET10 SDK. For the\n      full SQL Server stack use supported x64 hardware with enough memory for SQL Server.\n      WPF does not run in the Linux ASP.NET image. Accept applicable vendor licences\n      yourself during installation. Run docker version and docker compose version in a\n      terminal; start Docker Desktop if the engine cannot be reached.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Compare the boundaries</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        A virtual machine has its own guest operating-system kernel. Containers usually\n        share the host kernel while isolating processes, filesystems and networking. On\n        Windows/macOS, Docker Desktop uses a Linux environment for Linux containers.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        An image is layered and read-only. A container adds a writable layer and a\n        process. Deleting the container discards that layer, not a separately kept named\n        volume.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        A registry stores images. A repository groups related images; a tag identifies a\n        labelled version. A tag can move, while a digest identifies particular content.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        A container is not a guarantee of perfect security. Run least-privileged\n        processes, limit exposed ports, update images and avoid mounting the Docker socket\n        into ordinary applications.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>First commands</h2>\n    <pre><code>docker version\ndocker compose version\ndocker image ls\ndocker ps -a</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Expected: client/server version details, Compose version, local images and\n      containers. Exact versions/IDs differ. Cannot connect to daemon means Docker Desktop\n      or the engine is not ready.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>\n        Does rebuilding an image automatically replace a running container?\n      </summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. Create/recreate the container from the new image, for example docker compose\n        up --build -d.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Can a Windows WPF window run in this Linux API container?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. WPF is Windows desktop technology. Containerise the server/API parts using\n        supported images.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/docker-dotnet-image": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    A multi-stage build uses a large toolbox to build the app, then copies only the\n    finished app into a smaller runtime image.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prepare your computer</h2>\n    <p>\n      Install Docker Desktop with Linux containers enabled and the .NET10 SDK. For the\n      full SQL Server stack use supported x64 hardware with enough memory for SQL Server.\n      WPF does not run in the Linux ASP.NET image. Accept applicable vendor licences\n      yourself during installation. Run docker version and docker compose version in a\n      terminal; start Docker Desktop if the engine cannot be reached.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Read Api/Dockerfile line by line</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        FROM sdk:10.0 AS build includes the compiler and restore tools. WORKDIR chooses\n        the working folder.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        COPY StudyLab.csproj then RUN dotnet restore caches dependency download until\n        project references change. COPY . . brings in source.\n      </li>\n      <li>dotnet publish -c Release -o /out --no-restore creates deployment output.</li>\n      <li>\n        FROM aspnet:10.0 starts the smaller runtime stage. COPY --from=build copies\n        published output, not the whole SDK.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS=8080 chooses the container HTTP port. EXPOSE documents it;\n        it does not publish a host port.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        USER $APP_UID uses the non-root app account provided by the Microsoft image.\n        ENTRYPOINT starts StudyLab.dll.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Build from the correct folder</h2>\n    <pre><code>cd Api\ndotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate\ndocker build -t studylab-api:1 .</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Generate the migration once using the matching dotnet-ef tool before building; the\n      source project includes the SQL Server Design/provider packages. The dot at the end\n      is the build context. .dockerignore excludes bin,obj,.env and local databases.\n      Expected: build steps finish and studylab-api:1 appears in docker image ls.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why copy the csproj before all source files?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        A source-only change can reuse the dependency-restore layer instead of restoring\n        every time.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why does COPY fail when building from the repository root?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The Dockerfile paths are relative to its build context. Run from Api or pass the\n        intended context and Dockerfile paths.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/docker-monitor-scale": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    A working stack needs observation. Start by asking which service is failing, then\n    inspect that service’s logs and dependencies.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prepare your computer</h2>\n    <p>\n      Install Docker Desktop with Linux containers enabled and the .NET10 SDK. For the\n      full SQL Server stack use supported x64 hardware with enough memory for SQL Server.\n      WPF does not run in the Linux ASP.NET image. Accept applicable vendor licences\n      yourself during installation. Run docker version and docker compose version in a\n      terminal; start Docker Desktop if the engine cannot be reached.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Useful commands</h2>\n    <pre><code>docker compose ps\ndocker compose logs --tail 100 api\ndocker compose logs --tail 100 db\ndocker compose top\ndocker stats --no-stream\ndocker compose restart website\ndocker compose stop\ndocker compose start</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      ps shows state and published ports. logs shows startup/errors. stats shows resource\n      use. restart does not rebuild changed source; use up --build for that. stop pauses\n      the stack’s processes; down removes the stack containers/network but normally keeps\n      named volumes.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Scaling without a port conflict</h2>\n    <p>\n      The supplied api publishes one fixed host port5100, so blindly using --scale api=2\n      will cause a host-port collision. For a scaling exercise, copy the Compose file,\n      remove api’s host ports section, add expose:[\"8080\"], keep internal service-name\n      access and put a proper reverse proxy/load balancer in front. Then docker compose -f\n      compose.scale.yaml up -d --scale api=2 can create replicas. Use a separate reviewed\n      migration job before replicas start; do not let each production replica race a\n      startup migration.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Troubleshooting table in words</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>\n        db unhealthy: check password strength, memory, startup time, architecture support\n        and SQL logs.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        API migration error: verify generated migrations were copied into Api before image\n        build, and check connection/permissions.\n      </li>\n      <li>Website connection refused: wait for API readiness and check SchoolApi.</li>\n      <li>Port in use: change the host side or stop the conflicting process.</li>\n      <li>\n        No matching image architecture: choose supported hardware or a compatible database\n        alternative.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        File changes not visible: rebuild the image and recreate the service; restarting\n        an old image is not a rebuild.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>\n        Why can service_started still lead to a temporary website failure?\n      </summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        It means the process was started, not that migrations and dependency checks are\n        complete. Add/read readiness handling for a production design.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Should you solve every error by deleting volumes?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. Diagnose first. Deleting volumes can erase the evidence and the data without\n        fixing the underlying issue.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/docker-networking": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    Containers have their own idea of localhost. Service names are the addresses they use\n    to talk to one another on the Compose network.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prepare your computer</h2>\n    <p>\n      Install Docker Desktop with Linux containers enabled and the .NET10 SDK. For the\n      full SQL Server stack use supported x64 hardware with enough memory for SQL Server.\n      WPF does not run in the Linux ASP.NET image. Accept applicable vendor licences\n      yourself during installation. Run docker version and docker compose version in a\n      terminal; start Docker Desktop if the engine cannot be reached.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Map the addresses</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Browser on your computer → localhost:5200 → website container port8080.</li>\n      <li>\n        Website server → http://api:8080/ → API container. This is server-to-server HTTP,\n        so browser CORS does not apply to that internal hop.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        API → Server=db,1433 → SQL Server. The database port is not published to the host\n        in this sample.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        ConnectionStrings__School maps to ConnectionStrings:School in ASP.NET\n        configuration. Environment variables can override appsettings.json.\n      </li>\n      <li>\n        The .env file helps Compose substitute values. It is not an encrypted vault.\n        Docker inspect/config output can reveal environment values. Use managed secrets\n        and a least-privilege database account for production.\n      </li>\n    </ul>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n    <p>\n      LEFA\\SQLEXPRESS is a Windows host instance name, not the address of the db\n      container. Host port5100 and container port8080 have different jobs. Bind practice\n      published ports to127.0.0.1 to avoid exposing the lab on every network interface.\n      Inside containers, use service names; outside, use the published localhost port.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>\n        Website cannot reach localhost:5100 from its container. Fix the URL.\n      </summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        Set SchoolApi to http://api:8080/. The API is another container on the Compose\n        network.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Can a password be safe just because .env is hidden?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        No. It is plaintext. Keep it out of Git, logs and screenshots, and use proper\n        secret management for deployment.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/docker-volumes": "<div class=\"theory\">\n  <p>\n    A container is disposable. Your students’ records should not be. A named volume keeps\n    database files when the database container is recreated.\n  </p>\n  <section>\n    <h2>How to test this example in Visual Studio</h2>\n    <p>\n      Download the project below, extract it into its own folder, then open\n      <strong>StudyLab.csproj</strong>. Wait for NuGet restore. Choose Build → Build\n      Solution. For console projects choose Start Without Debugging. For API projects use\n      View → Terminal, make sure the terminal is in the folder containing StudyLab.csproj,\n      then run the command shown in the lesson. Do not paste all files into Program.cs:\n      use the exact file names displayed below.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prepare your computer</h2>\n    <p>\n      Install Docker Desktop with Linux containers enabled and the .NET10 SDK. For the\n      full SQL Server stack use supported x64 hardware with enough memory for SQL Server.\n      WPF does not run in the Linux ASP.NET image. Accept applicable vendor licences\n      yourself during installation. Run docker version and docker compose version in a\n      terminal; start Docker Desktop if the engine cannot be reached.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Prove persistence</h2>\n    <pre><code>docker compose down\ndocker compose up -d</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      Create a row before down. After up and readiness, read again. Expected: the row\n      remains because school-data survives a normal down. The volume is not the same as a\n      backup; losing/corrupting the volume can still lose data. Use SQL Server’s supported\n      backup/restore process and practise recovery.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Destructive cleanup is a separate choice</h2>\n    <pre><code>docker compose down -v</code></pre>\n    <p>\n      <strong>This deletes the practice database volume and its rows.</strong> Use only\n      when you intentionally want a fresh empty lab and have no needed data there. Do not\n      teach students to run it as the normal stop command. Removing containers and\n      deleting persistent data are different actions.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Development versus production</h2>\n    <p>\n      The example’s sa login and TrustServerCertificate are clearly labelled classroom\n      shortcuts. A real stack needs a restricted application login, trusted certificates,\n      reviewed migrations, backups, monitored storage and managed secrets. Do not scale a\n      database by copying its writable files into several independent containers.\n    </p>\n  </section>\n  <section>\n    <h2>Try it yourself</h2>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Why did all rows disappear after down -v?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>\n        The -v flag deletes named volumes managed by this stack. Restore from a real\n        backup if needed; recreating a volume does not recover old rows.\n      </p>\n    </details>\n    <details class=\"panel\">\n      <summary>Is keeping a volume sufficient disaster recovery?</summary>\n      <h3>Worked answer</h3>\n      <p>No. Back up and test restores to a different location/system.</p>\n    </details>\n  </section>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/helpful-resources": "<p class=\"eyebrow\">ANOTHER WAY TO UNDERSTAND</p>\n<h1>Helpful resources</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Explore explanations, demonstrations and practice from trusted learning providers.\n</p>\n<p id=\"resource-context\">{{dynamic_1}}</p>\n<div class=\"button-row\">\n  <a class=\"button\" href=\"{{dynamic_2}}\">Back to my studies</a\n  ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#study-break\">Take a break</a>\n</div>\n<label class=\"field-label\" for=\"resource-topic\">What would you like help with?</label\n><select id=\"resource-topic\">\n  <option value=\"csharp\">C# and ASP.NET Core</option>\n  <option value=\"docker\">Docker and Compose</option>\n  <option value=\"programming\">Programming foundations</option>\n  <option value=\"algorithms\">Arrays and algorithms</option>\n  <option value=\"sql\">SQL queries</option>\n  <option value=\"design\">Database design and normalisation</option>\n  <option value=\"joins\">Relationships and joins</option>\n  <option value=\"scripting\">SQL Server scripts and transactions</option>\n  <option value=\"hadoop\">Hadoop and MapReduce</option>\n</select>\n<div id=\"resource-cards\" class=\"resource-card-grid\"></div>\n<p>\n  Links open in a new tab so your study page stays here. External sites have their own\n  privacy policies; videos may use mobile data. SQL syntax can differ between SQL Server,\n  SQLite and other systems.\n</p>\n",
  "studylab/learning-progress": "<p><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"#backup\">Back up or restore progress</a></p>\n<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by progressPage(). -->\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">YOUR LEARNING RECORD</p>\n<h1>{{dynamic_1}} progress</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  {{dynamic_2}} of {{dynamic_3}} lessons complete. {{dynamic_4}}/{{dynamic_5}} quiz\n  questions currently correct; {{dynamic_6}} checked.\n</p>\n<p>\n  These are your latest checked answers, not a mastery score. Completion is marked from\n  each lesson after its required checks. Saved progress is local to this browser profile.\n</p>\n<div class=\"button-row\">{{dynamic_7}}</div>\n{{dynamic_8}}{{dynamic_9}}\n<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Reset completion marks</h2>\n  <p>\n    This resets completion marks for all learning modules, including older saved marks.\n    Quiz answers, drafts and exam attempts remain available.\n  </p>\n  <button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"reset-completion\">Reset completion marks</button>\n  <p id=\"progress-reset-status\" role=\"status\"></p>\n</section>\n<dialog id=\"progress-reset-dialog\">\n  <h2>Reset all completion marks?</h2>\n  <p>Your saved answers and drafts will stay available.</p>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"keep-completion\">Keep marks</button\n    ><button class=\"button danger\" id=\"confirm-reset-completion\">Reset marks</button>\n  </div>\n</dialog>\n",
  "studylab/programming-downloads": "<p>\n  <a class=\"text-link\" href=\"/learn/study-packs/\">Explore the new Study Packs preview</a>\n</p>\n<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by resourcesPage(). -->\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">LEARN ONLINE OR TAKE IT WITH YOU</p>\n<h1>Your study resources.</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Download editable Word practice packs, printable PDFs and a teaching PowerPoint for each\n  learning unit. Use the same exercise numbers to match questions with answers.\n</p>\n<section class=\"panel download-panel\">\n  <h2>Practice question and answer packs</h2>\n  <p>\n    229 exercises: 110 quiz questions, 22 debugging challenges, 44 lesson tasks and 53\n    task-library exercises. Includes arrays, parallel arrays, IF/ELSE and SWITCH/CASE.\n  </p>\n  <form id=\"download-pack\">\n    <div class=\"download-controls\">\n      <label class=\"field-label\"\n        >What would you like?<select id=\"pack-content\">\n          <option value=\"questions\">Questions only</option>\n          <option value=\"questions-and-answers\">Questions and answers</option>\n          <option value=\"answers\">Answers only</option>\n        </select></label\n      ><label class=\"field-label\"\n        >File format<select id=\"pack-format\">\n          <option value=\"pdf\">PDF (.pdf)</option>\n          <option value=\"docx\">Word (.docx)</option>\n        </select></label\n      ><a\n        id=\"pack-download\"\n        class=\"button\"\n        href=\"./downloads/logiclab-questions.pdf\"\n        download\n        >Download questions PDF</a\n      >\n    </div>\n  </form>\n  <p class=\"note\">\n    These are open revision downloads. Answer packs are available directly. If you want to\n    test yourself first, choose Questions only. Written solutions are examples and other\n    correct approaches may exist.\n  </p>\n</section>\n<div class=\"section-heading\">\n  <h2>Learning unit slides</h2>\n  <p>Editable PowerPoint (.pptx)</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"resource-intro\">\n  The detailed decks cover all lesson theory in each unit, definitions before use,\n  declared variables, numbered pseudocode walkthroughs, step-by-step traces and worked\n  exercise solutions. Each unit also has a short 10-slide revision deck with key\n  definitions, worked examples, practice answers and common mistakes. Unit 2 includes\n  IF/ELSE and SWITCH/CASE. Unit 3 includes single and two-dimensional arrays as well as\n  parallel arrays.\n</p>\n<div class=\"slides-grid\">{{dynamic_1}}</div>\n<div class=\"note\">\n  PowerPoint files can be opened in PowerPoint or compatible presentation software.\n  Downloads work without an account. This is supplementary practice material.\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/programming-home": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by dashboard(). -->\n<section class=\"welcome\">\n  <p class=\"eyebrow\">PROGRAMMING LOGIC & DESIGN</p>\n  <h1>What would you like to learn?</h1>\n  <p class=\"lede\">\n    Choose a topic. Read the explanation, follow an example, then try it yourself.\n  </p>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <a class=\"button\" href=\"#lesson/lesson-1\">Start with the basics</a\n    ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#practice\">Practise a topic</a>\n  </div>\n</section>\n{{dynamic_1}}\n<section class=\"course-topics\">\n  <div class=\"section-heading\">\n    <h2>Explore the course</h2>\n    <p>Start anywhere. Learn at your own pace.</p>\n  </div>\n  {{dynamic_2}}\n</section>\n",
  "studylab/programming-lesson-2": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by lessonPage(). -->\n<div class=\"lesson-header\">\n  <p class=\"eyebrow\">UNIT 0{{dynamic_1}} / GUIDED LESSON</p>\n  <h1>{{dynamic_2}}</h1>\n  <p class=\"lede\">Understand it. Try it. Explain why it works.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"lesson-tabs\" role=\"tablist\" aria-label=\"Lesson sections\">{{dynamic_3}}</div>\n<div class=\"lesson-layout\">\n  <section id=\"lesson-panel\" class=\"panel\" role=\"tabpanel\"></section>\n  <aside\n    class=\"panel objectives\"\n    style=\"position: static; height: auto; overflow: visible\"\n  >\n    <h3>Learning goals</h3>\n    <ul>\n      {{dynamic_4}}\n    </ul>\n    <div class=\"sidebar-divider\"></div>\n    <a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#unit/{{dynamic_5}}\">Back to unit {{dynamic_6}}</a>\n  </aside>\n</div>\n<nav class=\"lesson-paging\" aria-label=\"Lesson navigation\">{{dynamic_7}}{{dynamic_8}}</nav>\n<div class=\"button-row\">\n  <button id=\"print-lesson\" class=\"button secondary\">\n    Print / save this view as PDF\n  </button>\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/programming-lesson": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by lessonPage(). -->\nPRLD5121 / Unit {{dynamic_1}}\n",
  "studylab/programming-practice": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by practiceHub(). -->\n<h1>A little practice helps it stick.</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Pick the way you want to practise. Answers and hints are available after an attempt.\n</p>\n<div class=\"hub-grid\">{{dynamic_1}}</div>\n<p class=\"note\">Looking for a topic quiz? Open any lesson and choose “Quiz”.</p>\n",
  "studylab/programming-unit": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by unitPage(). -->\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">PROGRAMMING LOGIC &amp; DESIGN / UNIT 0{{dynamic_1}}</p>\n<h1>{{dynamic_2}}</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  {{dynamic_3}}. Each lesson includes theory, worked examples, five quiz questions, an\n  error-finding challenge and two practice tasks.\n</p>\n<section class=\"panel unit-overview-panel\">\n  <h2>Unit overview</h2>\n  <p>\n    {{dynamic_4}}. Work through the lessons below, then use their quizzes and worked tasks\n    to check your understanding.\n  </p>\n  <h3>Key themes</h3>\n  <ul>\n    {{dynamic_5}}\n  </ul>\n  <details>\n    <summary>Learning outcomes for this unit</summary>\n    <ul>\n      {{dynamic_6}}\n    </ul>\n  </details>\n  <h3>Suggested study order</h3>\n  <p>\n    Read the theory and worked examples, try the quiz, find the pseudocode error, then\n    write your own solution. Use diagrams and tracing when you want to see the logic in\n    action.\n  </p>\n</section>\n<div class=\"button-row\">\n  <a class=\"button\" href=\"./downloads/logiclab-unit-{{dynamic_7}}.pptx?v=15\" download\n    >Download Unit {{dynamic_8}} PowerPoint</a\n  ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#resources\">Word and PDF practice packs</a>\n</div>\n<div class=\"lesson-list\">{{dynamic_9}}</div>\n<div class=\"note\">\n  You can learn in any order. Try the theory before attempting the exercises.\n</div>\n",
  "studylab/progress-backup": "<p class=\"eyebrow\">YOUR PROGRESS . YOUR COPY</p>\n<h1>Back up or restore progress</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Save a copy of your StudyLab work, then restore it in another browser or on another\n  device. No account is needed.\n</p>\n<p class=\"note\">\n  This is manual backup, not automatic syncing. Download a fresh file after doing more\n  work. Keep it private because it can contain your written answers and code. Live SQL\n  database contents and unsaved edits are not copied. Exam timers do not pause during\n  backup; a restored timed exam may already have expired.\n</p>\n<p id=\"backup-status\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>1. Download your progress</h2>\n  <p>\n    Includes saved activities and drafts, completed lesson entries, checked quiz records,\n    practice exam records and C#/Docker lesson notes. Only StudyLab learning data is\n    included.\n  </p>\n  <label\n    ><input id=\"backup-export-settings\" type=\"checkbox\" /> Include my theme and display\n    settings</label\n  >\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <button id=\"backup-export\" class=\"button\" type=\"button\">\n      Download progress backup</button\n    ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#saved-work\">Review saved work</a>\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    Transfer the downloaded .json file to the other device, for example through your own\n    drive or a cable. Open this page there and choose the file below.\n  </p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>2. Choose a backup to preview</h2>\n  <label for=\"backup-file\">StudyLab progress backup (.json, up to 8 MB)</label>\n  <input id=\"backup-file\" type=\"file\" accept=\".json,application/json\" />\n  <p>\n    The file is read in this browser. Choosing it does not upload it to a server or change\n    progress.\n  </p>\n  <div id=\"backup-preview\" hidden>\n    <h3>Backup preview</h3>\n    <button id=\"backup-refresh\" class=\"button secondary\" type=\"button\">\n      Refresh preview\n    </button>\n    <p id=\"backup-date\"></p>\n    <p id=\"backup-summary\"></p>\n    <label for=\"backup-mode\">Restore option</label\n    ><select id=\"backup-mode\">\n      <option value=\"merge\">Merge - keep existing progress and add backup data</option>\n      <option value=\"replace\">\n        Replace - use the backup instead of current learning data\n      </option>\n    </select>\n    <p id=\"backup-mode-help\"></p>\n    <label id=\"backup-settings-choice\" hidden\n      ><input id=\"backup-restore-settings\" type=\"checkbox\" /> Also restore the display\n      settings from this file</label\n    >\n    <p id=\"backup-result-summary\"></p>\n    <div id=\"backup-conflicts\"></div>\n    <h3>3. Protect your current work, then restore</h3>\n    <p>\n      Download a safety copy of this browser's current progress before applying changes.\n      This safety copy includes display settings so you can recover them too.\n    </p>\n    <button id=\"backup-safety\" class=\"button secondary\" type=\"button\">\n      Download current progress safety backup\n    </button>\n    <label class=\"backup-confirmation\"\n      ><input id=\"backup-confirm\" type=\"checkbox\" disabled /><span\n        id=\"backup-confirm-label\"\n        >I have saved the safety backup and want to apply this restore.</span\n      ></label\n    >\n    <button id=\"backup-apply\" class=\"button\" type=\"button\" disabled>\n      Restore and reload StudyLab\n    </button>\n    <p>\n      StudyLab reloads after a successful restore to use the restored records. If you\n      change saved work in another tab after this preview, preview the file again before\n      restoring.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n</section>\n",
  "studylab/pseudocode-diagram-lab": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by labPage(). -->\n<div class=\"lab-header\">\n  <p class=\"eyebrow\">FROM ALGORITHM TO PICTURE</p>\n  <h1>See the logic take shape.</h1>\n  <p class=\"lede\">\n    Convert the documented pseudocode syntax into a flowchart or a module call hierarchy.\n    Processing happens in your browser; no AI API is used.\n  </p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"lab-controls\">\n  <label class=\"field-label\"\n    >Load an example<select id=\"lab-example\">\n      {{dynamic_1}}\n    </select></label\n  ><label class=\"field-label\"\n    >Diagram type<select id=\"lab-type\">\n      <option value=\"flow\">Flowchart</option>\n      <option value=\"hierarchy\">Module hierarchy</option>\n    </select></label\n  >\n</div>\n<div class=\"lab-grid\">\n  <section class=\"panel\">\n    <h2>Your pseudocode</h2>\n    <label class=\"field-label\" for=\"pseudocode\"\n      >Edit the algorithm<textarea\n        id=\"pseudocode\"\n        class=\"editor\"\n        spellcheck=\"false\"\n        maxlength=\"100000\"\n      >\n{{dynamic_2}}</textarea\n      >\n    </label>\n    <div class=\"button-row\">\n      <button class=\"button\" id=\"generate-chart\">Generate diagram</button\n      ><button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"trace-my-code\">Trace this code</button>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"status\" id=\"lab-status\" role=\"status\"></p>\n    <label class=\"field-label\"\n      >Flowchart section<select id=\"lab-module\">\n        <option value=\"main\">Main program</option>\n      </select></label\n    >\n  </section>\n  <section class=\"panel\">\n    <h2>Your diagram</h2>\n    <div class=\"diagram-toolbar\">\n      <button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"download-svg\" disabled>Download SVG</button\n      ><button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"download-png\" disabled>Download PNG</button\n      ><button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"diagram-pdf\" disabled>Print / PDF</button\n      ><button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"fit-chart\" disabled>Fit width</button>\n    </div>\n    <div id=\"lab-diagram\" class=\"diagram\">\n      <div class=\"diagram-placeholder\">\n        <span>◇</span>\n        <p>Your diagram will appear here.<br />Choose an example and generate it.</p>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </section>\n</div>\n<details class=\"syntax panel\">\n  <summary>Supported syntax and limits</summary>\n  <p>\n    The converter checks recognised statements and matching block boundaries. It does not\n    execute expressions, validate types or prove that an algorithm is correct. Keywords\n    are case-insensitive. Use one statement per line.\n  </p>\n  <div class=\"scroll-table\">\n    <table>\n      <thead>\n        <tr>\n          <th>Structure</th>\n          <th>Notation</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr>\n          <td>Program</td>\n          <td><code>BEGIN</code> ... <code>END</code></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td>Input and output</td>\n          <td><code>INPUT name</code> / <code>OUTPUT \"Hello\", name</code></td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td>Assignment and declaration</td>\n          <td>\n            <code>SET total = total + value</code> / <code>DECLARE total AS REAL</code>\n          </td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td>Selection</td>\n          <td>\n            <code>IF condition THEN</code> ... <code>ELSE</code> ... <code>ENDIF</code>.\n            Use ELSEIF condition THEN for additional branches, or nest IF inside ELSE.\n          </td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td>Counted loop</td>\n          <td>\n            <code>FOR i = 1 TO 5 STEP 1</code> ... <code>ENDFOR</code>. STEP must be a\n            nonzero integer; it defaults to 1.\n          </td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td>Conditional loops</td>\n          <td>\n            <code>WHILE condition DO</code> ... <code>ENDWHILE</code> /\n            <code>REPEAT</code> ... <code>UNTIL condition</code>\n          </td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td>CASE</td>\n          <td>\n            <code>CASE expression OF</code>, <code>WHEN value:</code>,\n            <code>OTHERWISE:</code>, <code>ENDCASE</code>. Displayed as equivalent chained\n            decisions.\n          </td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td>Methods</td>\n          <td>\n            <code>METHOD name(parameters)</code> ... <code>ENDMETHOD</code> ,\n            <code>MODULE name(parameters)</code> ... <code>ENDMODULE</code> or\n            <code>FUNCTION name(parameters) RETURNS REAL</code> ...\n            <code>ENDFUNCTION</code>. Define methods outside BEGIN/END.\n          </td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td>Calls and returns</td>\n          <td>\n            <code>CALL name(arguments)</code>. Hierarchy view also recognises defined\n            function names inside expressions. RETURN creates a terminal exit in a\n            flowchart. Select a method in the section selector to inspect its body.\n          </td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td>Comments</td>\n          <td>\n            Use <code>//</code> for whole-line or inline comments outside quoted strings.\n            Maximum 1,000 non-empty lines, 100,000 characters and 16 levels of nesting\n            protect browser responsiveness.\n          </td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    Also accepted: PRINT/DISPLAY, READ, START/STOP, END IF, ELSE IF, plain assignments\n    using = or arrows, FOR ... FROM ... TO, NEXT and WEND. There is no quota or payment\n    per diagram. Structured syntax and browser safety limits still apply.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    Hierarchy charts show call relationships, including recursion; uncalled methods are\n    shown separately. Undefined calls are reported. SWITCH / CASE is accepted as an alias\n    for CASE ... OF, WHEN and OTHERWISE. Class syntax, overload resolution, BREAK and\n    CONTINUE are not supported in this release. Use the class diagrams in Unit 5 for\n    object-oriented examples.\n  </p>\n</details>\n",
  "studylab/refresh-games": "<section class=\"panel refresh-games\" aria-labelledby=\"refresh-game-title\">\n  <h2 id=\"refresh-game-title\">{{dynamic_1}}</h2>\n  <p id=\"refresh-instructions\">{{dynamic_2}}</p>\n  <div id=\"refresh-controls\" class=\"button-row\"></div>\n  <p id=\"refresh-status\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p>\n  <div id=\"refresh-board\"></div>\n  <div id=\"refresh-extra\"></div>\n</section>\n",
  "studylab/saved-work": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by savedWorkPage(). -->\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">CONTINUE WHERE YOU LEFT OFF</p>\n<h1>Your saved work</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Drafts and question progress save on this browser profile. They are shared by anyone\n  using this same profile, and do not sync to another device. Private browsing, clearing\n  site data or browser storage limits can remove or prevent saving.\n</p>\n<p>\n  Tracer code is saved as a draft. Enter inputs and prepare a fresh run after reopening.\n  Runtime inputs and trace history are not saved.\n</p>\n<div class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>Saved items</h2>\n  {{dynamic_1}}\n</div>\n<section class=\"panel saved-work-controls\">\n  <h2>Clear saved work</h2>\n  <p>\n    This removes drafts, exercise answers, quiz progress and saved hints from this\n    browser. Completed-lesson marks are kept. Use My progress to reset completion marks\n    separately.\n  </p>\n  <button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"clear-saved-work\">Clear saved work</button>\n  <p id=\"clear-work-status\" role=\"status\"></p>\n</section>\n<dialog id=\"clear-work-dialog\" aria-labelledby=\"clear-work-title\">\n  <h2 id=\"clear-work-title\">Remove all saved work?</h2>\n  <p>\n    This removes this site's saved drafts and question progress in the current browser\n    profile. Copy any work you want to keep first.\n  </p>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"cancel-clear-work\">Keep saved work</button\n    ><button class=\"button danger\" id=\"confirm-clear-work\">Remove saved work</button>\n  </div>\n</dialog>\n",
  "studylab/settings-panel": "<!-- Settings content. Behaviour: js/shared/theme-settings.mjs. Themes: display-preferences.mjs. -->\n<div class=\"settings-heading\">\n  <div>\n    <p class=\"eyebrow\">MAKE STUDYLAB YOURS</p>\n    <h2 id=\"settings-title\">Your study space</h2>\n    <p>Make yourself comfortable. Preview changes on the page as you go.</p>\n  </div>\n  <button\n    type=\"button\"\n    class=\"settings-close\"\n    id=\"settings-close\"\n    aria-label=\"Close settings\"\n  >\n    <svg\n      aria-hidden=\"true\"\n      viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"\n      width=\"20\"\n      height=\"20\"\n      fill=\"none\"\n      stroke=\"currentColor\"\n      stroke-width=\"1.8\"\n      stroke-linecap=\"round\"\n    >\n      <path d=\"m6 6 12 12M18 6 6 18\" />\n    </svg>\n  </button>\n</div>\n<nav class=\"settings-category-nav\" aria-label=\"Settings sections\">\n  <button type=\"button\" data-settings-category=\"themes\" aria-pressed=\"true\">\n    Themes\n  </button>\n  <button type=\"button\" data-settings-category=\"reading\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\n    Reading\n  </button>\n  <button type=\"button\" data-settings-category=\"editor\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\n    Editor\n  </button>\n  <button type=\"button\" data-settings-category=\"learning\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\n    Learning\n  </button>\n</nav>\n<section class=\"settings-section\" aria-labelledby=\"settings-themes-title\">\n  <h3 id=\"settings-themes-title\">Choose your theme</h3>\n  <p>Keep it light, revisit a vintage favourite, or explore twelve colourful palettes.</p>\n  <label class=\"settings-collection\" for=\"settings-theme-collection\"\n    >Theme collection<select id=\"settings-theme-collection\">\n      <option value=\"all\">All themes (22)</option>\n      <option value=\"colourful\">Colourful (12 new)</option>\n      <option value=\"vintage\">Vintage (6)</option>\n      <option value=\"classic\">Classic</option>\n    </select></label\n  >\n  <p class=\"settings-collection-note\">\n    Light is always available. Your selected theme stays active when browsing collections.\n  </p>\n  <fieldset class=\"theme-choice-grid\">\n    <legend class=\"settings-sr-only\">StudyLab theme</legend>\n    {{dynamic_1}}\n  </fieldset>\n</section>\n<section class=\"settings-section\" aria-labelledby=\"settings-reading-title\">\n  <h3 id=\"settings-reading-title\">Reading comfort</h3>\n  <div class=\"settings-fields\">\n    <label for=\"settings-text-size\"\n      >Text size<select id=\"settings-text-size\">\n        <option value=\"small\">Small</option>\n        <option value=\"standard\">Standard</option>\n        <option value=\"large\">Large</option>\n        <option value=\"larger\">Extra large</option>\n      </select></label\n    >\n    <label for=\"settings-spacing\"\n      >Reading spacing<select id=\"settings-spacing\">\n        <option value=\"comfortable\">Comfortable</option>\n        <option value=\"relaxed\">Relaxed</option>\n        <option value=\"compact\">Compact</option>\n      </select></label\n    >\n    <label for=\"settings-width\"\n      >Page width<select id=\"settings-width\">\n        <option value=\"standard\">Standard</option>\n        <option value=\"focused\">Focused reading</option>\n        <option value=\"wide\">Wide workspace</option>\n      </select></label\n    >\n  </div>\n  <label class=\"settings-motion\"\n    ><input id=\"settings-reduce-motion\" type=\"checkbox\" /> Reduce animations and smooth\n    scrolling</label\n  >\n</section>\n<section class=\"settings-section\" aria-labelledby=\"settings-editor-title\">\n  <h3 id=\"settings-editor-title\">Code editor</h3>\n  <p>\n    Applies to SQL and pseudocode editors. These choices change how code looks, keeping\n    your code unchanged.\n  </p>\n  <div class=\"settings-fields\">\n    <label for=\"settings-editor-size\"\n      >Code text size<select id=\"settings-editor-size\">\n        <option value=\"standard\">Standard (14 px)</option>\n        <option value=\"large\">Large (16 px)</option>\n        <option value=\"larger\">Extra large (18 px)</option>\n      </select></label\n    >\n    <label for=\"settings-editor-wrap\"\n      >Long lines<select id=\"settings-editor-wrap\">\n        <option value=\"wrap\">Wrap to editor width</option>\n        <option value=\"scroll\">Scroll horizontally</option>\n      </select></label\n    >\n    <label for=\"settings-editor-tab-size\"\n      >Tab display width<select id=\"settings-editor-tab-size\">\n        <option value=\"2\">2 spaces</option>\n        <option value=\"4\">4 spaces</option>\n        <option value=\"8\">8 spaces</option>\n      </select></label\n    >\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    Tab width controls existing tab characters. The Tab key continues to move between\n    controls.\n  </p>\n</section>\n<section\n  class=\"settings-section settings-learning\"\n  aria-labelledby=\"settings-learning-title\"\n>\n  <h3 id=\"settings-learning-title\">Your learning</h3>\n  <p><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#backup\">Back up or restore progress</a></p>\n  <p><a href=\"#study-break\">Are you tired? Take a refreshing break</a></p>\n  <p><a href=\"#helpful-resources\">Find helpful learning resources</a></p>\n  <p>\n    Continue your work or review your progress. Changing display settings keeps your\n    answers and drafts.\n  </p>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <a class=\"button secondary\" id=\"settings-progress\" href=\"#progress/prld\"\n      >My progress</a\n    ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#saved-work\">Saved work</a\n    ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"../\">Lefa’s portfolio</a>\n  </div>\n</section>\n<div class=\"settings-bottom\">\n  <p id=\"settings-save-status\" role=\"status\"></p>\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"text-link\" id=\"settings-reset\">\n    Reset display settings\n  </button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"settings-storage-note\">\n  Saved on this browser and device with no expiry date. Clearing site data, private\n  browsing or browser storage restrictions can remove or prevent saving. Device appearance\n  follows your device setting when selected.\n</p>\n",
  "studylab/sql-workspace": "<!-- Edit this page layout here. Dynamic placeholders are filled by sqlPage(). -->\n<p class=\"breadcrumb\">\n  <a href=\"#db\">Databases</a><span>/</span><strong>SQL workspace</strong>\n</p>\n<p class=\"eyebrow\">WRITE . RUN . EXPLORE</p>\n<h1>Your SQL practice space.</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Choose your dialect and a fictional dataset. Write a query, run it and inspect the\n  results.\n</p>\n<div class=\"sql-engine-note\">\n  <strong>Browser practice engine: SQLite.</strong> SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL are\n  compatibility modes for the documented teaching subset, not live server connections.\n  <button type=\"button\" class=\"text-link\" id=\"sql-show-limits\">\n    See supported syntax and differences</button\n  >.\n</div>\n<div class=\"sql-toolbar\">\n  <label class=\"field-label\"\n    >SQL dialect<select id=\"sql-mode\">\n      {{dynamic_1}}\n    </select></label\n  ><label class=\"field-label\"\n    >Demo dataset<select id=\"sql-dataset\">\n      {{dynamic_2}}\n    </select></label\n  ><button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"sql-reset\">Reset demo data</button>\n</div>\n<p class=\"muted sql-session-note\">\n  Draft SQL saves separately for each challenge or sandbox dataset and dialect in this\n  browser. Table changes last only while this workspace stays open. A reload, module\n  change, reset, stop or timeout restores the demo.\n</p>\n<div class=\"sql-layout\">\n  <section class=\"panel sql-main\">\n    <div class=\"sql-example-picker\">\n      <label class=\"field-label\">Worked example<select id=\"sql-example\"></select></label\n      ><button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"sql-load\">Load example</button>\n    </div>\n    <p id=\"sql-example-help\" class=\"muted\"></p>\n    <label class=\"field-label\" for=\"sql-editor\">SQL editor</label\n    ><textarea\n      id=\"sql-editor\"\n      class=\"editor\"\n      rows=\"13\"\n      maxlength=\"30000\"\n      spellcheck=\"false\"\n      autocapitalize=\"off\"\n      autocomplete=\"off\"\n      autocorrect=\"off\"\n    >\n{{dynamic_3}}</textarea\n    >\n    <div class=\"button-row\">\n      <button class=\"button\" id=\"sql-run\" disabled>Run SQL</button\n      ><button class=\"button\" id=\"sql-check\" disabled>Check my answer</button\n      ><button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"sql-stop\" disabled>Stop query</button\n      ><button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"sql-download-query\">\n        Download query .sql\n      </button>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"muted sql-shortcut\">\n      Ctrl+Enter or ⌘+Enter runs your query. Up to 20 statements per run; results show up\n      to 500 rows per statement.\n    </p>\n    <p id=\"sql-status\" role=\"status\">Loading the local SQL engine...</p>\n    <p id=\"sql-grade-summary\" class=\"feedback\" role=\"status\"></p>\n    <div id=\"sql-results\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n  </section>\n  <aside\n    class=\"panel sql-schema\"\n    style=\"position: static; height: auto; overflow: visible\"\n  >\n    <h2>Explore the data</h2>\n    <p id=\"sql-data-description\"></p>\n    <div id=\"sql-schema\"></div>\n    <button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"sql-download-data\">\n      Download demo setup .sql\n    </button>\n    <p class=\"muted\">\n      Use the setup script in an empty database in your chosen native DBMS. The scripts\n      use explicitly supplied IDs.\n    </p>\n  </aside>\n</div>\n<section class=\"panel sql-challenges\">\n  <h2>Try a challenge</h2>\n  <label class=\"field-label\"\n    >Choose a task<select id=\"sql-task\">\n      <option value=\"\">Choose a challenge...</option>\n      {{dynamic_4}}\n    </select></label\n  >\n  <div id=\"sql-task-panel\">\n    <p class=\"muted\">\n      Select a challenge, write SQL in the editor, then choose Check my answer for\n      feedback or run an attempt before revealing the worked answer.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n</section>\n<details class=\"panel sql-limits\" id=\"sql-limits\">\n  <summary>Supported syntax and dialect differences</summary>\n  <p>\n    All queries run locally using sql.js (SQLite compiled to WebAssembly). This is an\n    introductory SQL practice sandbox. It is not Microsoft SQL Server, PostgreSQL or\n    MySQL.\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li>\n      Practise SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, DISTINCT, joins, subqueries, common-table\n      expressions, GROUP BY, HAVING, COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX, ROUND, COALESCE, INSERT,\n      UPDATE, DELETE, basic CREATE/ALTER/DROP TABLE, views and indexes supported by\n      SQLite.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      SQL Server mode adapts a whole-number TOP on one simple SELECT to a row limit, and\n      removes an unquoted dbo. prefix. SQL Server/MySQL DROP INDEX ... ON table is\n      adapted. PostgreSQL/MySQL use LIMIT. Literal strings and quoted names are not\n      rewritten.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      Generated IDs (IDENTITY, SERIAL, AUTO_INCREMENT), server administration, stored\n      procedures, triggers, native date/string functions and transaction commands are not\n      implemented. Use explicit primary-key values and native tools for those features.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      SQLite does not reproduce native DBMS type, precision, string-length, collation,\n      date, permission or optimiser behaviour. A query that runs here is not proof of\n      identical behaviour on a native server. Use portable GROUP BY lists; the sandbox\n      does not enforce every native grouping rule.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      Each run is atomic: a failed batch is rolled back. Foreign keys are enabled. Results\n      are capped at 500 rows per statement, queries time out after 4 seconds, and database\n      pages are limited to about 32 MB. Stop/timeout reloads the demo. Avoid huge cross\n      joins and recursive queries.\n    </li>\n    <li>\n      Each tab has its own in-memory database. Saved drafts still share the browser\n      profile, so this is practice, not a private student account or secure assessment.\n    </li>\n  </ul>\n  <div class=\"scroll-table\">\n    <table class=\"db-table\">\n      <caption>\n        Native dialect reference (not all syntax runs in this browser)\n      </caption>\n      <thead>\n        <tr>\n          <th>Feature</th>\n          <th>SQL Server</th>\n          <th>PostgreSQL</th>\n          <th>MySQL</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr>\n          <th>First five rows</th>\n          <td>SELECT TOP (5) ... ORDER BY ...</td>\n          <td>... ORDER BY ... LIMIT 5</td>\n          <td>... ORDER BY ... LIMIT 5</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <th>Generated key</th>\n          <td>INT IDENTITY(1,1)</td>\n          <td>INTEGER GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY</td>\n          <td>INT AUTO_INCREMENT</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <th>Identifier quoting</th>\n          <td>[StudentName]</td>\n          <td>\"StudentName\"</td>\n          <td>`StudentName`</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <th>String concatenation</th>\n          <td>CONCAT(a, b)</td>\n          <td>a || b</td>\n          <td>CONCAT(a, b)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <th>Drop index</th>\n          <td>DROP INDEX idx ON table_name</td>\n          <td>DROP INDEX idx</td>\n          <td>DROP INDEX idx ON table_name</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n  <a class=\"text-link\" href=\"#db-resources\">Official documentation and more resources</a>\n</details>\n<dialog id=\"sql-reset-dialog\" aria-labelledby=\"sql-reset-title\">\n  <h2 id=\"sql-reset-title\">Reload the demo dataset?</h2>\n  <p id=\"sql-reset-message\">\n    This removes changes made to the open practice database, including custom tables. Your\n    SQL draft is kept.\n  </p>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <button class=\"button secondary\" id=\"sql-reset-cancel\">Keep working</button\n    ><button class=\"button\" id=\"sql-reset-confirm\">Load fresh demo</button>\n  </div>\n</dialog>\n",
  "studylab/study-break": "<p class=\"eyebrow\">A LITTLE ROOM TO BREATHE</p>\n<h1>Are you tired?</h1>\n<p class=\"lede\">\n  Put the studying down for a moment. Enjoy a gentle game, stretch, or look away from the\n  screen.\n</p>\n<div class=\"button-row\">\n  <a class=\"button\" href=\"{{dynamic_1}}\">Back to my studies</a\n  ><a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"#helpful-resources\">Explore helpful resources</a>\n</div>\n<nav class=\"break-game-picker\" aria-label=\"Choose a break game\">\n  <button type=\"button\" data-break-game=\"garden\" aria-pressed=\"true\">Garden pairs</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" data-break-game=\"words\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\n    Computing word search\n  </button>\n  <button type=\"button\" data-break-game=\"snake\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Snake</button>\n  <button type=\"button\" data-break-game=\"sliding\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\n    Sliding puzzle\n  </button>\n  <button type=\"button\" data-break-game=\"tic\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Tic-tac-toe</button>\n</nav>\n<div id=\"refresh-game-host\" hidden></div>\n<section class=\"panel break-game\" aria-labelledby=\"break-game-title\">\n  <p class=\"eyebrow\">NO TIMER. NO PRESSURE.</p>\n  <h2 id=\"break-game-title\">Garden pairs</h2>\n  <p>\n    Find six matching pairs. Choose two cards to reveal them. Use Tab and Enter or Space\n    to play with a keyboard.\n  </p>\n  <div class=\"button-row\">\n    <button type=\"button\" class=\"button secondary\" id=\"break-restart\">New garden</button\n    ><button type=\"button\" class=\"button secondary\" id=\"break-hide\" hidden>\n      Turn these cards over\n    </button>\n  </div>\n  <p id=\"break-status\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">Choose your first card.</p>\n  <div class=\"garden-board\" id=\"garden-board\" aria-label=\"Garden matching cards\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"panel\">\n  <h2>A screen-free reset</h2>\n  <p>\n    Relax your shoulders, have a sip of water, and look at something farther away. Come\n    back whenever you feel ready.\n  </p>\n  <p>These games have no ads, accounts, leaderboards or effect on your study scores.</p>\n</section>\n",
  "portfolio/airport-management": "<div class=\"eyebrow green\">Application development project</div>\n<h2 id=\"case-title\">Airport Sky Base System</h2>\n<h3>The problem</h3>\n<p>\n  Airport information needs a usable interface that supports access to records and\n  operational workflows.\n</p>\n<h3>My contribution</h3>\n<p>\n  I developed and tested an airport management application focused on accessing and\n  managing airport information.\n</p>\n<h3>Technologies and approach</h3>\n<p>\n  Application interface design, information management and functional testing.\n  Implementation details are available in the repository.\n</p>\n<h3>What it delivers</h3>\n<p>\n  The project provides a user interface for working with airport information and\n  demonstrates application development and testing.\n</p>\n<h3>Current status</h3>\n<p>\n  This is a repository-based project; it is not presented as a live airport operations\n  system.\n</p>\n<div class=\"project-actions\">\n  <a\n    class=\"text-btn\"\n    href=\"https://github.com/LEFA-2329/Airport-Sky-Base-System\"\n    rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    >View repository</a\n  >\n</div>\n",
  "portfolio/bursaries-team-project": "<div class=\"eyebrow green\">Team project | Four contributors</div>\n<h2 id=\"case-title\">Burzarsnet</h2>\n<h3>The problem</h3>\n<p>\n  South African applicants need a way to find bursary and internship opportunities, while\n  administrators need to manage the information published.\n</p>\n<h3>My contribution</h3>\n<p>\n  I collaborated with three other contributors to develop the bursary and internship\n  website. This was a shared team delivery.\n</p>\n<h3>Technologies and approach</h3>\n<p>\n  Web application development, administrative content management and team collaboration.\n</p>\n<h3>What it delivers</h3>\n<p>\n  The team developed a platform for bursary and internship listings with administration\n  capabilities.\n</p>\n<h3>Current status</h3>\n<p>\n  My contribution is presented as part of a four-person team, without attributing the\n  entire implementation to me.\n</p>\n<div class=\"project-actions\">\n  <a\n    class=\"text-btn\"\n    href=\"http://burzarsnet.com\"\n    rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    >Visit website</a\n  >\n</div>\n",
  "portfolio/diopong-primary-school": "<div class=\"eyebrow green\">Live website | Active development</div>\n<h2 id=\"case-title\">Diopong Primary School</h2>\n<h3>The problem</h3>\n<p>\n  Families and learners need an accessible place to find school information and\n  supplementary learning resources in English and Sepedi.\n</p>\n<h3>My contribution</h3>\n<p>\n  I built the responsive public website and bilingual learning hub, connected grade,\n  subject and topic filters, and added quizzes, downloadable learning packs and\n  browser-generated achievement certificates.\n</p>\n<h3>Technologies and approach</h3>\n<p>\n  HTML, CSS and JavaScript; GitHub Pages hosting with GitHub Actions deployment. Worksheet\n  and certificate generation runs in the browser without learner accounts.\n</p>\n<h3>What it delivers</h3>\n<p>\n  The live site offers 160 bilingual activities, 20 per grade from Grade R to 7, PDF and\n  editable Word worksheets, separate answers, and quiz certificates for Grades 4-7 at a\n  score of at least 70%.\n</p>\n<h3>Current status</h3>\n<p>\n  The website is live and maintained as it develops. School-approved information is being\n  added as supplied. Learning content and Sepedi translations await teacher review; the\n  staff dashboard is deferred.\n</p>\n<div class=\"project-actions\">\n  <a\n    class=\"text-btn\"\n    href=\"https://diopong.lefamalatji.co.za/\"\n    rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    >Visit live website</a\n  >\n  <a\n    class=\"text-btn\"\n    href=\"https://github.com/LEFA-2329/diopong-primary-school\"\n    rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    >View repository</a\n  >\n</div>\n",
  "portfolio/helpdesk-hub": "<div class=\"eyebrow green\">API project | In development</div>\n<h2 id=\"case-title\">HelpDeskHub</h2>\n<h3>The problem</h3>\n<p>\n  A help desk needs a structured way to connect support tickets with requesters, assigned\n  staff, departments, comments and ticket history.\n</p>\n<h3>My contribution</h3>\n<p>\n  I built the ASP.NET Core REST API foundation, worked on ticket, department and user\n  endpoints, integrated ASP.NET Identity, and updated database relationships through\n  Entity Framework migrations.\n</p>\n<h3>Technologies and approach</h3>\n<p>\n  C#, ASP.NET Core, PostgreSQL, Entity Framework Core, ASP.NET Identity, DTOs and Swagger\n  for endpoint testing.\n</p>\n<h3>What it delivers</h3>\n<p>\n  The project has a working API foundation and relational data layer that support\n  continued ticket-workflow development.\n</p>\n<h3>Current status</h3>\n<p>\n  An MVC front end, workload-based assignment, notifications and reporting remain on the\n  roadmap. This is a development project, not a completed production service.\n</p>\n<div class=\"project-actions\"></div>\n",
  "portfolio/preschool-management": "<div class=\"eyebrow green\">Application and data management project</div>\n<h2 id=\"case-title\">Pre-School Management System</h2>\n<h3>The problem</h3>\n<p>\n  Managing learners, teachers, books and borrowing records requires consistent records and\n  a clear administrative interface.\n</p>\n<h3>My contribution</h3>\n<p>\n  My project brings these records into an administrative application with login, a\n  dashboard and create, read, update and delete operations.\n</p>\n<h3>Technologies and approach</h3>\n<p>\n  Admin dashboard, database-backed record management and CRUD workflows. The repository\n  provides the implementation details.\n</p>\n<h3>What it delivers</h3>\n<p>\n  The application combines key statistics and administrative record management in one\n  place.\n</p>\n<h3>Current status</h3>\n<p>Source code is available for review. No live production deployment is claimed here.</p>\n<div class=\"project-actions\">\n  <a\n    class=\"text-btn\"\n    href=\"https://github.com/LEFA-2329/Pre-school-management-system\"\n    rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    >View repository</a\n  >\n</div>\n",
  "portfolio/sepedi-english-research": "<div class=\"eyebrow green\">MSc research | In progress</div>\n<h2 id=\"case-title\">Sepedi-English text generation</h2>\n<h3>The problem</h3>\n<p>\n  Limited code-switched training data and vocabulary coverage make natural text generation\n  difficult for underrepresented languages such as Sepedi.\n</p>\n<h3>My contribution</h3>\n<p>\n  I investigate data-scarcity effects using Sepedi-English radio-news text, preparing the\n  corpus, training language models and comparing their generated outputs.\n</p>\n<h3>Technologies and approach</h3>\n<p>\n  Python and TensorFlow / Keras; text preprocessing and tokenisation; FNet and LSTM\n  experiments, with mBERT in the broader research scope. Consistent dataset splits and\n  BLEU / ROUGE evaluation support comparison.\n</p>\n<h3>What it delivers</h3>\n<p>\n  The work produces model comparisons and generated-text examples to examine the effects\n  of corpus size, English coverage and unknown tokens. I also co-authored published\n  research with Thipe I. Modipa on Sepedi-English generation using FNet.\n</p>\n<h3>Current status</h3>\n<p>\n  The MSc investigation is ongoing. Published work and continuing MSc experiments are\n  distinct research outputs.\n</p>\n<div class=\"project-actions\"></div>\n",
  "portfolio/store-hub": "<div class=\"eyebrow green\">E-commerce engineering project</div>\n<h2 id=\"case-title\">Store Hub</h2>\n<h3>The problem</h3>\n<p>\n  An e-commerce application needs to connect product discovery, customer support and\n  administrative information across different user roles.\n</p>\n<h3>My contribution</h3>\n<p>\n  I developed a multi-role PHP and PostgreSQL platform with AI-driven personalisation,\n  intelligent product search, a session-aware support chatbot and business intelligence\n  reporting.\n</p>\n<h3>Technologies and approach</h3>\n<p>\n  PHP application logic and a PostgreSQL relational database, with separate platform\n  responsibilities supported through multiple user roles.\n</p>\n<h3>What it delivers</h3>\n<p>\n  The project brings storefront and management capabilities together in one application.\n  Source code is available for reviewing the implementation.\n</p>\n<h3>Current status</h3>\n<p>\n  The repository is the reviewable project output; this portfolio does not present it as a\n  verified production deployment.\n</p>\n<div class=\"project-actions\">\n  <a\n    class=\"text-btn\"\n    href=\"https://github.com/LEFA-2329/Advanced-E-Commerce-Marketplace-Platform\"\n    rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    >Explore the repository</a\n  >\n</div>\n",
  "portfolio/studylab": "<div class=\"eyebrow green\">Live learning application . In development</div>\n<h2 id=\"case-title\">StudyLab</h2>\n<h3>The problem</h3>\n<p>\n  Students need a place to connect programming and database theory with practical\n  examples, check mistakes and revise independently.\n</p>\n<h3>My contribution</h3>\n<p>\n  I designed and built the lessons, quizzes, pseudocode parser and tracer, flowchart\n  tools, dependency-diagram builder, SQL workspace, automatic SQL answer checks and\n  browser-local progress tracking.\n</p>\n<h3>What it delivers</h3>\n<p>\n  Four learning modules cover Programming Logic and Design, Database Introduction,\n  Intermediate and Advanced. Advanced includes Hadoop, HDFS, YARN, MapReduce and Hive\n  lessons. The SQL workspace provides seven fictional datasets, 52 worked examples and 47\n  challenges. Students can also use practice exams, printable resources and editable study\n  packs where available.\n</p>\n<h3>Technologies and approach</h3>\n<p>\n  HTML page layouts, CSS and JavaScript modules. SQL runs locally using SQLite compiled to\n  WebAssembly in a worker. Drafts and progress use browser storage; GitHub Actions\n  publishes the static application.\n</p>\n<h3>Current status and limits</h3>\n<p>\n  Live supplementary learning material under active development. Browser SQL compatibility\n  modes are not native SQL Server, PostgreSQL or MySQL connections. Hadoop examples need a\n  separate configured environment. Native server and cluster validation remain pending.\n  Saved progress stays in the same browser profile and does not sync across devices.\n</p>\n<div class=\"project-actions\">\n  <a class=\"text-btn\" href=\"./learn/\">Open StudyLab</a>\n  <a class=\"text-btn\" href=\"./learn/#sql\">Try SQL practice</a>\n  <a class=\"text-btn\" href=\"./learn/#db-unit/209\">Explore Hadoop</a>\n  <a\n    class=\"text-btn\"\n    href=\"https://github.com/LEFA-2329/lefa-portfolio./tree/main/dist/learn\"\n    target=\"_blank\"\n    rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"\n    >View source code</a\n  >\n</div>\n"
};
